var e={"gynaecology-abnormal-uterine-bleeding":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`AUB is uterine-corpus bleeding abnormal in **frequency, regularity, duration or volume**.`,`It applies only to a non-pregnant woman of reproductive age.`,`Normal cycle (FIGO 2018): every **24-38 days**, lasting **8 days or less**.`,`Cycle-to-cycle variation of 7-9 days or less is normal.`,`**Heavy menstrual bleeding** is loss that impairs her quality of life, not a fixed 80 mL.`,`Drop menorrhagia, metrorrhagia and DUB; say HMB, intermenstrual bleeding and AUB-O.`,`NFHS-5: **57% of Indian women** aged 15-49 are anaemic, so HMB quickly causes iron deficiency.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use **FIGO PALM-COEIN**: PALM is structural, COEIN is non-structural.`,`PALM: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy and hyperplasia.`,`COEIN: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not otherwise classified.`,`Only **submucosal fibroids (types 0-2)** reliably cause bleeding.`,`A woman can carry two labels at once, for example AUB-L(SM); C.`,`Bleeding in pregnancy, before menarche or after menopause is **not AUB**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. In AUB-O there is no ovulation, so no corpus luteum and no progesterone.`,`2. **Unopposed oestrogen** builds a thick, fragile endometrium.`,`3. The lining outgrows its blood supply and breaks down piecemeal, giving irregular bleeding.`,`4. Over years, unopposed oestrogen leads to **hyperplasia** and cancer.`,`5. In AUB-E, excess local **fibrinolysis and prostaglandin E2** cause heavy regular periods.`,`6. This is why **tranexamic acid and NSAIDs** work in ovulatory HMB.`,`7. About **13% of HMB** is an inherited bleeding disorder, usually von Willebrand disease.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about pregnancy first and do a **urine pregnancy test**.`,`Quantify loss: night changes, flooding, **clots larger than a 1-rupee coin**, days missed.`,`**Regular heavy** periods suggest L, A, C or E; **irregular** bleeding suggests AUB-O.`,`Intermenstrual or postcoital bleeding points to the cervix or a polyp.`,`Coagulopathy screen: heavy **since menarche**, PPH, or surgical or dental bleeding.`,`Note drugs (IUCD, DMPA, warfarin, DOACs, tamoxifen) and her fertility wish.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check pulse, BP, **pallor**, BMI, thyroid, acanthosis, hirsutism and bruising.`,`Feel the abdomen for a pelvic mass you cannot get below.`,`On speculum, **biopsy any visible cervical lesion**; a normal smear is not reassurance.`,`A **bulky, soft, tender uterus** suggests adenomyosis.`,`An **irregular, firm uterus** suggests fibroids.`,`Skip speculum in a virgo intacta and use ultrasound instead.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**UPT and full blood count** for every woman with HMB.`,`Add ferritin if the FBC suggests iron deficiency; **under 15 microgram/L** means depleted.`,`TSH and prolactin only if symptoms or irregular cycles point to them.`,`**Transvaginal ultrasound** if the uterus is enlarged, a mass is felt or treatment fails.`,`**Pipelle endometrial biopsy** at **45 or over**, or younger with unopposed-oestrogen risk.`,`Postmenopausal endometrium of **4 mm or less** has an NPV for cancer of about 99%.`,`**D and C is not a diagnostic test**; it misses focal lesions.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**LNG-IUS 52 mg** is first-line if there is no cavity distortion; it cuts loss 70-95%.`,`Expect irregular spotting for 3-6 months after insertion.`,`If LNG-IUS is declined, use **tranexamic acid** or **mefenamic acid** during periods.`,`These non-hormonal options suit the woman **trying to conceive**.`,`Hormonal options: combined pill, or norethisterone 5 mg TDS from **day 5 to 26**.`,`For AUB-O, give **MPA 10 mg daily for 12-14 days** each month to protect the endometrium.`,`Correct anaemia with oral iron, 60-120 mg elemental iron daily or alternate days.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Review at **3 months** with a bleeding diary and her own quality-of-life rating.`,`DMPA, ormeloxifene or a GnRH agonist are second-line options.`,`**Leuprolide** is used only before surgery; add tibolone add-back beyond 3 months.`,`Procedures: hysteroscopic resection, endometrial ablation, uterine artery embolisation, myomectomy.`,`**Hysterectomy is last**, only after an LNG-IUS trial and endometrial assessment.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Acute AUB with shock or **Hb under 7 g/dL**: two cannulae, cross-match, transfuse, admit.`,`Acute bleed drugs: **MPA 20 mg TDS for 7 days** or a monophasic COC TDS for 7 days.`,`Positive pregnancy test with bleeding and pain is **ectopic** until excluded.`,`Postmenopausal bleeding is endometrial cancer until proved otherwise.`,`A fibroid growing after menopause suggests **sarcoma**; refer to gynae-oncology.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Urgent 2-week referral**: postmenopausal bleeding at 55 or over, cervical lesion, suspicious mass.`,`Refer to gynaecology for failed treatment at 3-6 months or fibroids over 3 cm.`,`Refer **atypical hyperplasia** on biopsy and a positive vWD screen to specialists.`,`Recheck Hb 4 weeks after starting iron; expect a rise of 1-2 g/dL.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for heavy and abnormal uterine bleeding`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`LNG-IUS 52 mg`,`Intrauterine, releases about 20 microgram/day`,`5 years for HMB`,`Spotting 3-6 months, mild acne`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g oral three times daily`,`Up to 4 days from day 1`,`Avoid in active VTE; halve in renal impairment`],[`Mefenamic acid`,`500 mg oral three times daily with food`,`3-5 days per period`,`Dyspepsia, GI bleed; stop after 3 failed cycles`],[`Combined pill (EE 30 + LNG 150)`,`1 tablet oral daily`,`21/7 or continuous`,`VTE, BP rise; check WHO MEC`],[`Norethisterone`,`5 mg oral three times daily`,`Day 5-26 each cycle`,`Bloating, mood change; luteal-only regimen useless`],[`Medroxyprogesterone (AUB-O)`,`10 mg oral daily`,`12-14 days each month`,`Bloating, breast tenderness`],[`DMPA`,`150 mg deep IM or 104 mg SC`,`Every 12 weeks`,`Irregular bleeding, delayed fertility`],[`Ormeloxifene`,`60 mg oral twice weekly, then weekly`,`12 weeks each phase`,`Delayed periods; avoid in PCOS, liver disease`],[`Leuprolide`,`3.75 mg IM monthly`,`3-6 months, preoperative only`,`Hot flushes, bone loss; tibolone add-back`],[`Ferric carboxymaltose`,`20 mg/kg IV, max 1000 mg`,`Single dose, may repeat weekly`,`Hypersensitivity, hypophosphataemia`]]},{heading:`PALM-COEIN at a glance`,columns:[`Category`,`Typical pattern`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Polyp`,`Intermenstrual and postcoital bleeding`,`Hysteroscopy or sonohysterography`],[`Adenomyosis`,`Heavy painful periods, bulky tender uterus`,`TVS or MRI (junctional zone over 12 mm)`],[`Leiomyoma`,`Heavy regular periods, pressure symptoms`,`TVS with FIGO type 0-8`],[`Malignancy and hyperplasia`,`Intermenstrual or postmenopausal bleeding`,`Endometrial biopsy`],[`Coagulopathy`,`Heavy since menarche, bruising`,`vWF antigen, ristocetin cofactor, factor VIII`],[`Ovulatory dysfunction`,`Irregular, unpredictable, prolonged`,`Clinical pattern, TSH, prolactin`],[`Endometrial`,`Regular heavy, no lesion`,`Diagnosis of exclusion`],[`Iatrogenic`,`Breakthrough bleeding`,`Drug and device history`],[`Not otherwise classified`,`Post-caesarean niche, AV malformation`,`Doppler TVS, hysteroscopy`]]},{heading:`FIGO 2018 normal menstruation`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Normal`,`Abnormal`],rows:[[`Frequency`,`24-38 days`,`Under 24 or over 38 days`],[`Regularity`,`Variation 7-9 days or less`,`Variation 10 days or more`],[`Duration`,`8 days or less`,`Over 8 days`],[`Volume`,`Normal for the woman`,`HMB: affects quality of life`]]}],remember:[`**PALM-COEIN**: PALM structural, COEIN non-structural; two labels can coexist.`,`Urine pregnancy test before everything in a reproductive-age woman.`,`**Biopsy at 45**, or younger with obesity, PCOS or chronic anovulation.`,`LNG-IUS is first-line for HMB.`,`Norethisterone runs **day 5 to 26**; the luteal-only regimen is the classic wrong answer.`,`Trying to conceive: tranexamic acid or mefenamic acid only.`,`13% of HMB is a bleeding disorder; screen before surgery.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - abnormal uterine bleeding (PALM-COEIN)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Uterine-corpus bleeding abnormal in **frequency, regularity, duration or volume** in a non-pregnant woman of reproductive age; **acute, chronic (most of last 6 months) or intermenstrual**. **HMB = loss that impairs quality of life** (not the 80 mL research cut-off).`],[`Normal cycle (FIGO 2018)`,`Frequency **24-38 days**, variation **7-9 days or less**, duration **8 days or less**, volume normal for her. Drop menorrhagia, metrorrhagia and **DUB** - say HMB, IMB, AUB-O.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**FIGO PALM-COEIN**: **PALM structural** - Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma (**types 0-2 submucosal bleed**), Malignancy/hyperplasia; **COEIN non-structural** - Coagulopathy, Ovulatory, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not classified (niche, AVM). Multiple labels allowed, e.g. AUB-L(SM); C.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Adolescence and perimenopause (anovulation), **PCOS, hypothyroidism, hyperprolactinaemia, obesity**, fibroids, **vWD (13% of HMB)**, warfarin/DOACs, IUCD, tamoxifen; cancer risk - **45 or over, obesity, PCOS, tamoxifen, Lynch**. **57% Indian women anaemic** (NFHS-5).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Anovulation -> no corpus luteum -> **unopposed oestrogen** -> thick fragile endometrium -> piecemeal breakdown (AUB-O) -> **hyperplasia**; AUB-E: excess **tPA fibrinolysis and PGE2/prostacyclin** -> why **tranexamic acid and NSAIDs** work.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Pregnancy first (UPT)**; quantify loss - **night changes, flooding, clots larger than a 1-rupee coin**; regular heavy (L, A, C, E) vs irregular (O); IMB/PCB (cervix, polyp); **coagulopathy screen** - heavy since menarche, PPH, surgical bleeding; drugs; fertility wish.`],[`Key examination`,`Pulse, BP, **pallor**, BMI, thyroid, acanthosis/hirsutism, bruising; abdomen for a pelvic mass; **speculum - biopsy any visible lesion**; bimanual - bulky tender (adenomyosis) vs irregular firm (fibroid). No speculum in virgo intacta - use USG.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**UPT, FBC** (ferritin if low Hb; **under 15 microgram/L = depleted**); TSH/prolactin only if indicated; vWF panel if screen positive; **TVS** if mass or failure; **outpatient hysteroscopy** for suspected polyp/submucosal fibroid; **Pipelle at 45 or over** or under 45 with unopposed-oestrogen risk.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Pattern plus PALM-COEIN label; **PMB: endometrial thickness 4 mm or less** has NPV about 99%, over 4 mm needs tissue. **Not AUB**: pregnancy, pre-menarche, postmenopausal bleeding (cancer pathway). **No D and C as diagnosis**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**LNG-IUS 52 mg** first (cuts loss 70-95%, 5 years for HMB) if no structural cause or fibroids under 3 cm not distorting cavity; else **tranexamic acid 1 g TDS up to 4 days** (cuts 40-50%), **mefenamic acid 500 mg TDS** (25-35%), **COC EE 30 + LNG 150** or **norethisterone 5 mg TDS day 5-26**.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`AUB-O: **MPA 10 mg daily 12-14 days/month**; DMPA 150 mg IM 12-weekly; **ormeloxifene 60 mg twice weekly x 12 weeks then weekly**; **leuprolide 3.75 mg monthly preoperatively** with tibolone add-back beyond 3 months; ulipristal restricted (liver).`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Hysteroscopic polypectomy/myomectomy (types 0-2), **endometrial ablation** (family complete, normal cavity, under 10 weeks), UAE, myomectomy; **hysterectomy last** - only after LNG-IUS trial and endometrial assessment.`],[`Acute AUB (emergency)`,`ABC, 2 wide-bore cannulae, cross-match, **transfuse if Hb under 7**; **MPA 20 mg TDS x 7 days** or **monophasic COC TDS x 7 days**; IV CEE 25 mg 4-6 hourly; **tranexamic acid 10 mg/kg IV (max 600 mg/dose) 8-hourly**; Foley balloon tamponade, suction curettage.`],[`Special groups`,`**Adolescent**: anovulation common but **screen for vWD**; **trying to conceive**: tranexamic acid/NSAID only; renal impairment: **halve tranexamic acid**; anticoagulated: continue anticoagulant, add LNG-IUS or tranexamic acid with care; **perimenopause**: biopsy at 45 or over.`],[`Iron`,`Oral **60-120 mg elemental iron daily or alternate days** for 3 months after Hb normalises; **IV ferric carboxymaltose 20 mg/kg (max 1000 mg)** if Hb under 8 with ongoing loss, intolerance or surgery near; recheck Hb at 4 weeks (rise 1-2 g/dL).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**2-week pathway**: PMB 55 or over, visible cervical lesion, suspicious pelvic mass; refer for failed 3-6 months treatment, fibroids over 3 cm or cavity distortion, **atypical hyperplasia**; **admit** acute AUB with Hb under 7 or shock; haematology if vWD.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review at **3 months** with bleeding diary and QoL; check LNG-IUS threads at 4-6 weeks; continue LNG-IUS or COC long term; stop tranexamic acid/NSAID outside periods.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Pregnancy test before everything**; **LNG-IUS is first-line for HMB**; luteal-only norethisterone is useless; **biopsy at 45**; only **submucosal fibroids** reliably cause HMB; **13% of HMB = bleeding disorder**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`AUB in the OPD - from history to label`,steps:[{label:`Urine pregnancy test`,detail:`Miscarriage and ectopic are the bleeding diagnoses that kill`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Regular heavy or irregular?`,detail:`Regular heavy = ovulatory (L, A, C, E); irregular unpredictable = AUB-O`,tone:`decision`},{label:`FBC, then ferritin if Hb low`,detail:`Ferritin under 15 microgram/L = depleted stores; TSH and prolactin only if indicated`},{label:`Coagulopathy screen positive?`,detail:`Heavy since menarche, PPH, surgical or dental bleeding -> vWF antigen, ristocetin cofactor, factor VIII`,tone:`decision`},{label:`TVS or outpatient hysteroscopy`,detail:`Mass, enlarged uterus, IMB, failed treatment; hysteroscopy for suspected polyp or submucosal fibroid`},{label:`Pipelle biopsy if 45 or over`,detail:`Or under 45 with obesity, PCOS, chronic anovulation, tamoxifen, Lynch, or failed treatment`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Assign PALM-COEIN label(s)`,detail:`e.g. AUB-L(SM); AUB-O; AUB-C - then treat by fertility wish`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`HMB treatment ladder (NICE NG88)`,steps:[{label:`LNG-IUS 52 mg`,detail:`First-line if no cavity distortion; 70-95% less loss; spotting 3-6 months; 5 years for HMB`,tone:`good`},{label:`Tranexamic acid or NSAID`,detail:`TXA 1 g TDS up to 4 days from day 1; mefenamic acid 500 mg TDS with food; OK when trying to conceive`},{label:`COC or cyclical progestogen`,detail:`EE 30 + LNG 150 daily; norethisterone 5 mg TDS day 5-26; AUB-O: MPA 10 mg 12-14 days/month`},{label:`GnRH agonist (preoperative only)`,detail:`Leuprolide 3.75 mg IM monthly 3-6 months; tibolone 2.5 mg add-back beyond 3 months`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Procedures`,detail:`Hysteroscopic resection (types 0-2), ablation, UAE, myomectomy`},{label:`Hysterectomy - last resort`,detail:`Only after LNG-IUS trial (or reason declined) and endometrial assessment`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Look-alikes - which AUB category?`,columns:[`Feature`,`AUB-O`,`AUB-L (submucosal)`,`AUB-A`,`AUB-C`],rows:[[`Pattern`,`Irregular, unpredictable, prolonged`,`Heavy regular, IMB`,`Heavy painful regular`,`Heavy since menarche`],[`Clue`,`PCOS, thyroid, adolescence, perimenopause`,`Firm irregular uterus`,`Bulky tender globular uterus, parous 35-50`,`PPH, bruising, epistaxis, family history`],[`Test`,`Clinical, TSH, prolactin, biopsy if 45+`,`TVS FIGO type, hysteroscopy`,`TVS or MRI (JZ 12 mm or more)`,`vWF antigen, RCo, factor VIII`],[`First treatment`,`Cyclical MPA or COC`,`Hysteroscopic resection`,`LNG-IUS`,`Tranexamic acid, haematology`]]}]},"gynaecology-contraception":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Contraception is the intentional prevention of pregnancy by **reversible or permanent** methods.`,`Effectiveness is pregnancies per 100 women in the first year, typical versus perfect use.`,`The gap between perfect and typical use is **user error**.`,`**LARC** (implant, LNG-IUS, copper IUCD) is most effective because it removes user error.`,`NFHS-5: female sterilisation **37.9%** versus vasectomy **0.3%**; unmet need 9.4%.`,`National basket: Nirodh, **Mala-N**, **Chhaya**, **Antara**, Cu-IUCD, emergency pill, sterilisation.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**WHO MEC category 1**: no restriction on use.`,`**Category 2**: advantages generally outweigh the risks.`,`**Category 3**: risks usually outweigh advantages; use only if nothing else is acceptable.`,`**Category 4**: unacceptable health risk; do not use.`,`At ASHA or ANM level, categories 1-2 mean use and 3-4 mean do not use.`,`Some conditions differ for starting and continuing; IUCD with current PID is 4 to insert, 2 to continue.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Oestrogen suppresses FSH, so no dominant follicle is selected.`,`2. Progestogen suppresses the **LH surge** and thickens cervical mucus.`,`3. Together they abolish ovulation; the 7 pill-free days are the weak point.`,`4. Copper ions are **spermicidal and ovotoxic**; the IUCD is not an abortifacient.`,`5. Sperm survive up to 5 days, so emergency contraception within 120 hours still works.`,`6. Ethinylestradiol raises clotting factors, explaining **VTE risk** with combined pills.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask her goal: **spacing or limiting**, breastfeeding status and partner's views.`,`Use the **WHO pregnancy checklist** to be reasonably sure she is not pregnant.`,`Ask specifically about **migraine with aura**; it is category 4 for combined methods.`,`Note smoking at 35 or over, hypertension, past DVT and breast cancer.`,`Ask about enzyme inducers: rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, efavirenz.`,`Ask privately about partner control of contraception (reproductive coercion).`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Blood pressure** is the one essential check before the combined pill.`,`No pelvic, breast or Pap examination is needed before pills, injectables or implants.`,`Before an IUCD, do a speculum and **bimanual examination** for size, position and tenderness.`,`Sound the uterus; a normal depth is **6-9 cm**.`,`An unrecognised **retroverted uterus** is the classic setting for perforation.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`No routine blood tests are needed before any method in a healthy woman.`,`Urine pregnancy test only when the WHO checklist cannot exclude pregnancy.`,`Missing IUCD threads: pregnancy test, then **ultrasound**, then abdominal X-ray.`,`After vasectomy, **semen analysis at 12 weeks** must show azoospermia.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Counsel with **GATHER** or BCS+; quote effectiveness first, then let her choose.`,`**Mala-N**: one tablet daily, 21 hormonal and 7 iron, no break between packs.`,`Start the pill on days 1-5 with no back-up; otherwise use condoms for 7 days.`,`Missed pills: **1-2 missed**, take one and continue; **3 or more**, add condoms 7 days.`,`**Antara (DMPA) 150 mg** deep IM every 13 weeks; up to 4 weeks late without back-up.`,`**Chhaya 30 mg** twice weekly for 3 months, then once weekly; safe in breastfeeding.`,`**Cu-T 380A** lasts 10 years; LNG-IUS gives contraception up to 8 years.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Copper IUCD within 5 days** is the most effective emergency contraception.`,`**Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg** single dose ideally within 72 hours, up to 120 hours.`,`With BMI over 26, weight over 70 kg or enzyme inducers, give 3 mg or prefer IUCD.`,`**Ulipristal 30 mg** within 120 hours; wait 5 days before a hormonal method.`,`With enzyme inducers, switch to IUCD, LNG-IUS or DMPA rather than doubling pills.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Postpartum: POP, implant and DMPA can start **immediately** (DMPA now category 2).`,`Breastfeeding: no combined pill before 6 months.`,`IUCD postpartum within 48 hours or from 4-6 weeks; **48 hours to 4 weeks is category 3**.`,`Post-abortion: ovulation returns within 2 weeks, so start any method **the same day**.`,`Perimenopause: continue until 2 years after the last period if under 50, 1 year if over 50.`,`Sterilisation: woman 22-49, one child over 1 year; **spouse consent is not required**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Pill warning signs **ACHES**: abdominal pain, chest pain, headache, eye problems, severe leg pain.`,`Pelvic pain with a late period in an IUCD user is **ectopic** until proved otherwise.`,`PID with an IUCD: treat without removing it; remove if no response in 48-72 hours.`,`Pregnancy with an IUCD under 12 weeks with visible threads: **remove the device**.`,`Vasectomy needs condoms for 3 months until azoospermia is confirmed.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Contraceptive methods and doses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mala-N (LNG 0.15 + EE 0.03 mg)`,`1 tablet oral daily, 21 + 7 iron`,`Continuous packs`,`Nausea, spotting, VTE, BP rise`],[`Desogestrel POP 75 microgram`,`1 tablet oral daily`,`No break; late if over 12 h`,`Irregular bleeding, ovarian cysts`],[`Centchroman (Chhaya) 30 mg`,`Oral twice weekly, then weekly`,`3 months, then indefinitely`,`Delayed periods; avoid in PCOS, liver disease`],[`DMPA (Antara) 150 mg`,`Deep IM`,`Every 13 weeks`,`Amenorrhoea, weight gain, BMD loss, delayed fertility`],[`DMPA-SC 104 mg`,`SC, self-injection possible`,`Every 13 weeks`,`As DMPA IM; do not rub site`],[`Etonogestrel implant 68 mg`,`Subdermal, inner non-dominant arm`,`3 years`,`Unpredictable bleeding, acne`],[`Copper IUCD 380A`,`Intrauterine`,`10 years`,`Heavier painful periods, expulsion, perforation`],[`LNG-IUS 52 mg`,`Intrauterine`,`Up to 8 years`,`Spotting 3-6 months; category 4 in breast cancer`],[`Levonorgestrel EC 1.5 mg`,`Oral single dose`,`Within 72 h, up to 120 h`,`Nausea; 3 mg if BMI over 26`],[`Ulipristal acetate 30 mg`,`Oral single dose`,`Within 120 h`,`Wait 5 days before hormonal method`]]},{heading:`WHO MEC categories for common conditions`,columns:[`Condition`,`Combined pill`,`POP / implant`,`DMPA`,`Cu-IUCD`],rows:[[`Migraine with aura`,`4`,`2`,`2`,`1`],[`Age 35 or over, 15 or more cigarettes a day`,`4`,`1`,`1`,`1`],[`BP 160/100 or over`,`4`,`2`,`3`,`1`],[`History of DVT or PE`,`4`,`2`,`2`,`1`],[`Breastfeeding under 6 weeks`,`4`,`2`,`2 (was 3)`,`Under 48 h: 1`],[`Current breast cancer`,`4`,`4`,`4`,`1`],[`Rifampicin or enzyme-inducing anticonvulsants`,`3`,`3 (implant 2)`,`1`,`1`],[`Current PID or purulent cervicitis`,`1`,`1`,`1`,`4 to insert`]]},{heading:`Effectiveness tiers (typical-use failure per 100 in year 1)`,columns:[`Tier`,`Methods`,`Failure rate`],rows:[[`Tier 1`,`Implant, LNG-IUS, copper IUCD, sterilisation`,`Under 1`],[`Tier 2`,`Injectables, pills, LAM`,`4-7`],[`Tier 3`,`Condoms, fertility awareness, withdrawal`,`13-20`],[`Tier 4`,`Spermicides`,`About 21`]]}],remember:[`MEC: **1-2 use, 3-4 do not use** at peripheral level.`,`Migraine with aura: combined pill category 4, copper IUCD category 1.`,`Most effective emergency contraception is the **copper IUCD within 5 days**.`,`Only BP is needed before the combined pill; no pelvic exam or blood test.`,`**ACHES** are the pill warning signs.`,`WHO MEC 2025: DMPA in breastfeeding under 6 weeks is now **category 2**.`,`Vasectomy: condoms 3 months and azoospermia on semen analysis at 12 weeks.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO MEC 6th edition (2025): about 2000 recommendations reaffirmed and 112 new or updated - tags moved from WHO MEC 2015 to WHO MEC 2025.`,`WHO MEC 2025: DMPA/NET-EN in breastfeeding women under 6 weeks postpartum is now category 2 (was 3) - all progestogen-only methods can start immediately postpartum.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - contraception and WHO MEC`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Intentional prevention of pregnancy by reversible or permanent methods; effectiveness = **pregnancies per 100 women in first year (typical vs perfect use)** or **Pearl index**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Effectiveness tiers**: tier 1 under 1/100 - **implant 0.1, LNG-IUS 0.1-0.2, Cu-IUCD 0.8, sterilisation**; tier 2 - DMPA 4, pill 7; tier 3 - condom 13. **WHO MEC 2025 (6th ed)**: **1 no restriction, 2 benefits outweigh risks, 3 risks usually outweigh, 4 do not use**; field rule **1-2 use, 3-4 do not**.`],[`National basket (India)`,`**Nirodh, Mala-N** (LNG 0.15 + EE 0.03 + 7 iron), **Chhaya** centchroman 30 mg, **Antara** DMPA 150 mg IM, **Cu-T 380A (10 y) / Cu 375 (5 y)**, PPIUCD, EC pill, sterilisation; implant and DMPA-SC being added. NFHS-5: mCPR **56.5%**, female sterilisation **37.9%**, vasectomy **0.3%**, unmet need **9.4%**.`],[`Mechanisms`,`COC: oestrogen suppresses FSH + progestogen blocks LH surge -> **no ovulation**; POP (LNG/NET): **cervical mucus** (3 h window); desogestrel, implant, DMPA: **inhibit ovulation**; Cu-IUCD: **spermicidal, ovotoxic** (not abortifacient); centchroman: **SERM, out-of-phase endometrium**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Reproductive goal (space/limit), **breastfeeding and weeks postpartum**, **migraine with aura**, **35 or over and smoking**, BP, VTE, breast cancer, liver disease, **enzyme inducers (rifampicin, carbamazepine, efavirenz)**, lamotrigine, STI risk, IPV/reproductive coercion; **WHO pregnancy checklist**.`],[`Key examination`,`**BP before CHC is the only essential examination**; no pelvic, breast, Pap or blood test before pills, DMPA, implant, condoms. Before IUCD: speculum (cervicitis), **bimanual (retroversion)**, sound **6-9 cm**.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**None routine**; UPT only if checklist fails (repeat at 3 weeks after quick start); STI test before IUCD only if high individual risk (insert same day); **semen analysis 12 weeks after vasectomy**; USG/X-ray for missing threads.`],[`MEC must-knows`,`**CHC category 4**: migraine with aura, **35+ smoking 15+/day**, **BP 160/100+**, DVT/PE, current breast cancer, **breastfeeding under 6 weeks**, postpartum under 21 days. **IUCD category 4 to insert** with current PID/cervicitis (2 to continue). **Breastfeeding under 6 weeks: POP, implant and DMPA all category 2 (WHO 2025)**.`],[`Treatment - pills`,`**Mala-N 1 daily**, start day 1-5 (else 7 days condoms); **1-2 missed: take one, continue; 3 or more: + condoms 7 days**, skip iron week if week 3, **EC if week 1 + sex in last 5 days**; POP late if **over 3 h** (desogestrel **12 h**); **Chhaya twice weekly x 3 months then weekly**.`],[`Injectables & LARC`,`**DMPA 150 mg deep IM every 13 weeks** (2 weeks early to **4 weeks late**), DMPA-SC 104 mg self-injection; **implant 68 mg ENG 3 years**, inner non-dominant arm 8-10 cm above epicondyle; **LNG-IUS up to 8 years** (5 for HMB/HRT); Cu-T 380A 10 years.`],[`Emergency contraception`,`**LNG 1.5 mg within 72 h** (to 120 h; **3 mg if BMI over 26/weight over 70 kg or enzyme inducer**); **ulipristal 30 mg within 120 h** (wait 5 days before hormones); **Cu-IUCD within 5 days - most effective (failure under 0.1%)**; pregnancy test in 3 weeks if period late.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Cu-T: **NSAID before, sound, load under 5 min, withdraw the tube (do not push rod)**, cut threads 3-4 cm; **PPIUCD within 10 min of placenta or 48 h** with Kelly forceps to fundus; no-scalpel vasectomy; minilap Pomeroy or Falope ring (6 weeks postpartum).`],[`Special groups`,`**Postpartum**: Cu-IUCD within 48 h or after 4 weeks, **POP/implant/DMPA immediately**, COC not before 6 months if breastfeeding (21 days if not); **post-abortion**: any method same day; **perimenopause**: 2 years after LMP if under 50, 1 year if over 50; **adolescents**: all methods eligible; **HIV**: efavirenz lowers implant/pill efficacy.`],[`Complications`,`COC: **VTE (2 -> 5-7/10 000)**, stroke, BP rise; DMPA: BMD loss, **fertility delay about 10 months**, weight 1-2 kg/y; IUCD: perforation about 1/1000, expulsion 3-5%, heavier periods; implant: unpredictable bleeding, deep insertion.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**ACHES** on CHC (abdominal pain, chest pain, headache/aura, eye, severe leg pain) - stop same day; missing threads + positive UPT - exclude ectopic; non-palpable implant; PID within 20 days of insertion (treat, keep device unless no response 48-72 h).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Pills: **1 year supply**, BP yearly; IUCD: one visit at **3-6 weeks**; DMPA: next dose 13 weeks; pregnancy with IUCD under 12 weeks and threads visible - **remove**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**GATHER/BCS+ counselling**, Mission Parivar Vikas, Nayi Pehel kits, ASHA home delivery; **sterilisation eligibility 22-49, one child over 1 year, spouse consent not required**; Family Planning Indemnity Scheme.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO MEC 6th edition (Nov 2025)** reaffirmed the 2015 categories and changed 112 - **DMPA under 6 weeks breastfeeding now category 2 (was 3)**; WHO SPR 4th edition issued alongside.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Migraine with aura: **COC 4, Cu-IUCD 1**; **best EC = Cu-IUCD**; DMPA grace **4 weeks late**; vasectomy needs **azoospermia at 12 weeks**; **BP is the only test before the pill**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Choosing and starting a method in one visit`,steps:[{label:`Reproductive goal and preferences`,detail:`Space or limit, breastfeeding, partner, past experience, fears; offer LARC to all`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Reasonably not pregnant? (WHO checklist)`,detail:`7 days or less since period start, no sex since LMP, correct method use, under 4 weeks postpartum, LAM`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Screen MEC conditions`,detail:`Migraine aura, 35+ smoker, BP, VTE, breast cancer, enzyme inducers, postpartum weeks`,tone:`warn`},{label:`BP if CHC; pelvic exam only for IUCD`,detail:`No Pap, breast exam or bloods needed for pills, DMPA, implant`},{label:`Quick start today`,detail:`Outside day 1-5 (day 1-7 DMPA/implant): condoms 7 days; Cu-IUCD effective at once`},{label:`Teach use, side effects, ACHES, return`,detail:`Bleeding changes explained halve discontinuation; dual protection if STI risk`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Postpartum contraception by timing (WHO MEC 2025)`,columns:[`Method`,`Breastfeeding under 6 weeks`,`6 weeks to 6 months`,`Not breastfeeding`],rows:[[`Combined pill`,`4`,`3`,`Not before 21 days (3-4 earlier)`],[`POP, implant`,`2 - start immediately`,`1`,`1 from delivery`],[`DMPA / NET-EN`,`2 (was 3 in 2015) - start immediately`,`1`,`1 from delivery`],[`Cu-IUCD`,`Under 48 h: 1; 48 h-4 weeks: 3; 4 weeks+: 1`,`1`,`Same timing rule`],[`LNG-IUS`,`Under 48 h: 2; 48 h-4 weeks: 3; 4 weeks+: 1`,`1`,`Same timing rule`],[`LAM`,`Effective if fully breastfeeding, amenorrhoeic, under 6 months`,`Ends at 6 months`,`Not applicable`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Emergency contraception - which one?`,root:`Unprotected sex or method failure - how long ago?`,arms:[{label:`Within 72 hours`,tone:`neutral`,steps:[`LNG 1.5 mg once`,`3 mg if BMI over 26/weight over 70 kg or enzyme inducer`,`Start method same day, condoms 7 days`]},{label:`72-120 hours`,tone:`decision`,steps:[`Ulipristal 30 mg once`,`Wait 5 days before hormonal method`,`LNG still has some effect`]},{label:`Within 5 days, any time`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Cu-IUCD - most effective (failure under 0.1%)`,`Also within 5 days of earliest ovulation`,`Gives 10 years of contraception`]}]}]},"gynaecology-pcos":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`PCOS is a lifelong **endocrine-metabolic disorder**, not just an ovarian cyst.`,`**Rotterdam criteria**: any **2 of 3** after excluding mimics.`,`The three are ovulatory dysfunction, hyperandrogenism, and PCOM on ultrasound or raised AMH.`,`Irregular cycles in adults: under 21 or over 35 days, or fewer than 8 a year.`,`**PCOM**: 20 or more follicles of 2-9 mm, or ovarian volume 10 mL or more.`,`India: prevalence **3.7-22.5%**; use Asian cut-offs (BMI 23, 25; waist over 80 cm).`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Phenotype A**: all three features, with the worst metabolic risk.`,`**Phenotype B**: hyperandrogenism plus anovulation.`,`**Phenotype C**: hyperandrogenism plus PCOM, still ovulating.`,`**Phenotype D**: anovulation plus PCOM without hyperandrogenism, the mildest.`,`Adolescents need **both** irregular cycles and hyperandrogenism; otherwise label them 'at risk'.`,`No ultrasound or AMH for diagnosis **within 8 years of menarche**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Fast **GnRH pulses** favour LH over FSH.`,`2. LH drives over-active theca cells to make excess androgen.`,`3. **Insulin resistance** (50-70%, even lean women) causes hyperinsulinaemia, which acts like LH.`,`4. Insulin lowers **SHBG**, so free testosterone rises.`,`5. Androgens and high AMH arrest follicles at 2-9 mm; no dominant follicle, no ovulation.`,`6. No progesterone means **unopposed oestrogen**, giving hyperplasia and endometrial cancer risk.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Take a menstrual history since menarche, including the longest gap without a period.`,`Ask about excess hair, acne, scalp thinning and how she removes hair.`,`**Rapid onset** with voice change or clitoral enlargement suggests an androgen-secreting tumour.`,`Ask her **fertility plans**; this decides the COC versus ovulation induction.`,`Look for mimics: galactorrhoea, hypothyroid symptoms, striae and bruising.`,`Screen mood (PHQ-9, GAD-7), eating disorders and snoring.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **BMI, waist** and blood pressure at every visit.`,`**Modified Ferriman-Gallwey score**: nine areas graded 0-4; 4-6 or more is hirsutism in South Asians.`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** on the neck and axillae marks insulin resistance.`,`**Virilisation** (clitoromegaly, deep voice, temporal balding) is not PCOS; work up for tumour.`,`Look for Cushingoid features: purple striae, proximal myopathy, bruising.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Exclude mimics with **TSH, prolactin and morning 17-OHP**; beta-hCG if amenorrhoeic.`,`17-OHP over 2 ng/mL needs an ACTH stimulation test for non-classical CAH.`,`Total testosterone with SHBG to calculate the free androgen index.`,`Testosterone **over 150 ng/dL** or DHEAS over twice normal: image ovaries and adrenals.`,`**75 g OGTT** at diagnosis and every 1-3 years; HbA1c misses IGT.`,`Fasting lipid profile at diagnosis.`,`Do not order the **LH:FSH ratio** or fasting insulin.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Lifestyle first**: 5-10% weight loss within 6 months often restores ovulation.`,`Diet deficit of 500-750 kcal/day; no special PCOS diet is superior.`,`Exercise at least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity.`,`**COC** is first-line for cycles and hirsutism; it lowers LH and raises SHBG.`,`**Metformin** for metabolic features, titrated to 1500-2000 mg/day with meals.`,`Not on a COC: **MPA 10 mg for 12-14 days** every 1-3 months to protect the endometrium.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add **spironolactone 25-100 mg** if hirsutism persists after 6 months of COC.`,`Spironolactone needs reliable contraception because it feminises a male fetus.`,`**Letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7** is first-line ovulation induction, up to 7.5 mg.`,`Clomiphene 50 mg days 2-6 is the alternative, up to 150 mg, maximum 6 ovulatory cycles.`,`Confirm ovulation with mid-luteal progesterone over 10 nmol/L and follicle tracking.`,`Third line: gonadotrophins (WHO 2025 prefers them over ovarian drilling), then IVF.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Adolescents 'at risk': treat symptoms and reassess 8 years after menarche.`,`Pregnancy: higher risk of **GDM**, pre-eclampsia and preterm birth; do an early OGTT.`,`PCOS is the strongest risk factor for **OHSS** during gonadotrophin cycles.`,`Letrozole for ovulation induction is **off-label in India**; document consent.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Weight, waist, BP and mood every 6-12 months.`,`Ensure a withdrawal bleed **at least every 3 months** if not on a COC.`,`Refer urgently for rapid virilisation or suspected Cushing syndrome.`,`Refer to fertility services after 6 ovulatory cycles without pregnancy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in PCOS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`COC (EE 30 + LNG 150)`,`1 tablet oral daily`,`Review hirsutism at 6 months`,`Nausea, BP rise, VTE; check WHO MEC`],[`Metformin`,`500 mg oral daily, titrate to 1500-2000 mg/day`,`Long term`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency; stop if eGFR under 30`],[`Medroxyprogesterone acetate`,`10 mg oral daily`,`12-14 days every 1-3 months`,`Bloating; not contraceptive`],[`Spironolactone`,`25-100 mg oral daily`,`After 6 months of COC`,`Hyperkalaemia; needs contraception`],[`Eflornithine 11.5% cream`,`Topical to face twice daily`,`Effect in 6-8 weeks`,`Burning, acne, rash`],[`Letrozole`,`2.5 mg oral days 3-7, up to 7.5 mg`,`Up to 6 ovulatory cycles`,`Hot flushes, headache; off-label in India`],[`Clomiphene citrate`,`50 mg oral days 2-6, up to 150 mg`,`Max 6 ovulatory cycles`,`Visual blurring (stop), multiple pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Rotterdam criteria and exclusions`,columns:[`Element`,`How it is established`,`Note`],rows:[[`Ovulatory dysfunction`,`Cycles under 21 or over 35 days`,`Or fewer than 8 a year`],[`Hyperandrogenism`,`mFG 4-6 or more, acne, free androgen index`,`Clinical or biochemical is enough`],[`PCOM or AMH`,`20 follicles 2-9 mm or volume 10 mL`,`Not within 8 years of menarche`],[`Exclude thyroid and prolactin`,`TSH, prolactin`,`Both mimic PCOS`],[`Exclude non-classical CAH`,`Morning follicular 17-OHP`,`Over 2 ng/mL needs ACTH test`],[`Exclude tumour or Cushing`,`Testosterone, DHEAS, dexamethasone test`,`Triggered by rapid virilisation`]]}],remember:[`Rotterdam = **2 of 3** and a diagnosis of exclusion (TSH, prolactin, 17-OHP).`,`Never order the LH:FSH ratio; it is not a criterion.`,`PCOM is now **20 follicles or 10 mL**.`,`5-10% weight loss before any drug.`,`**Letrozole beats clomiphene** for live birth in PCOS.`,`OGTT at diagnosis and every 1-3 years.`,`Refuses the pill? Still protect the endometrium with progestogen or LNG-IUS.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`WHO infertility guideline (Nov 2025): after failed letrozole, gonadotrophins preferred over laparoscopic ovarian drilling; letrozole alone preferred over letrozole plus metformin.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - polycystic ovary syndrome`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Lifelong **endocrine-metabolic disorder** of ovulatory dysfunction and hyperandrogenism with insulin resistance; diagnosis of **exclusion of mimics** (thyroid, prolactin, NC-CAH, tumour, Cushing). India prevalence **3.7-22.5%**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Rotterdam (2023 International Guideline) - 2 of 3**: oligo/anovulation, clinical/biochemical hyperandrogenism, **PCOM (20 or more follicles 2-9 mm or volume 10 mL or more) or raised AMH**; **phenotypes A-D** (A most metabolic, D mildest). **Adolescents: need both irregular cycles and hyperandrogenism; no USG/AMH within 8 years of menarche**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Polygenic (DENND1A, LHCGR, FSHR), prenatal androgen programming, **central obesity, thin-fat Asian phenotype**; Asian cut-offs **BMI 23 overweight, 25 obese, waist over 80 cm**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Fast GnRH pulses -> high LH** -> theca androgen + **hyperinsulinaemia (co-gonadotrophin) -> low SHBG -> high free testosterone** -> high AMH blocks FSH -> follicular arrest 2-9 mm -> anovulation -> **unopposed oestrogen -> hyperplasia**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Cycles since menarche (**under 21 or over 35 days, fewer than 8/year**), hair removal method, acne, alopecia; **rapid virilisation = tumour**; weight trajectory; **fertility plans now or later**; mood (PHQ-9), eating disorder, snoring; drugs (valproate).`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, **waist**, BP; **modified Ferriman-Gallwey (4-6 or more in South Asians)**, acne, Ludwig alopecia, **acanthosis nigricans**; virilisation (clitoral index over 35 mm2); Cushingoid striae; galactorrhoea, goitre.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**TSH, prolactin, 17-OHP** (over 2 ng/mL -> ACTH test), hCG if amenorrhoeic; **total testosterone + SHBG (FAI)**; **75 g OGTT + lipids** at diagnosis, repeat 1-3 yearly; TVS only if needed; **do not order LH:FSH, fasting insulin, HOMA-IR**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`2 of 3 Rotterdam after exclusions; **tumour triggers: testosterone over 150 ng/dL or DHEAS over twice ULN** with rapid virilisation; endometrial biopsy if amenorrhoea over 3-4 months, thick endometrium, persistent irregular bleeding.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Lifestyle for all: 5-10% weight loss in 6 months**, 500-750 kcal deficit, **150 min/week** exercise; **COC (EE 20-30 + LNG) for cycles and hirsutism** after MEC check; **cyclical MPA 10 mg 12-14 days every 1-3 months** if no COC; **metformin 500 mg -> 1500-2000 mg/day** for metabolic features.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`Hirsutism after **6 months COC: add spironolactone 25-100 mg** with contraception (feminises male fetus); eflornithine 11.5% cream BD, laser; **GLP-1 RA** for obesity (stop semaglutide 2 months before conception); no flutamide; inositol experimental.`],[`Fertility (ovulation induction)`,`**Letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7 -> up to 7.5 mg**, first-line (higher live birth than clomiphene, off-label in India - consent); clomiphene 50-150 mg days 2-6 if letrozole unusable (**with metformin, WHO 2025**); max **6 ovulatory cycles**; then **gonadotrophins (preferred over drilling, WHO 2025)**, then IVF.`],[`Special groups`,`**Adolescent 'at risk'**: treat symptoms, reassess 8 years after menarche; **pregnancy**: early OGTT, GDM, pre-eclampsia, preterm; **OHSS risk highest in PCOS** - cancel if 3 or more follicles 16 mm or more.`],[`Complications`,`**T2DM (about 4-fold)**, GDM, dyslipidaemia, NAFLD, OSA, hypertension, **endometrial hyperplasia/cancer (2-6 fold)**, depression/anxiety 3-5 fold, infertility.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Rapid virilisation, testosterone over 150 ng/dL, DHEAS over 2x ULN**, Cushing features - urgent; fertility referral after **6 ovulatory cycles** without pregnancy or other factor; severe depression or eating disorder.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Weight, waist, BP and mood every 6-12 months; **OGTT every 1-3 years**; lipids by CV risk; **ensure a bleed at least every 3 months**; review hirsutism at 6 months.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`International PCOS Guideline 2023 stands (PCOM **20 follicles**, AMH alternative in adults); **WHO infertility guideline 2025** endorses letrozole first, prefers gonadotrophins over drilling.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**No USG for PCOS within 8 years of menarche**; LH:FSH ratio is not a criterion; **letrozole beats clomiphene**; metformin is an adjunct, not an inducer; spironolactone only with contraception.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Diagnosing PCOS in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Irregular cycles or hyperandrogenism`,detail:`Cycles under 21 or over 35 days or fewer than 8/year; mFG 4-6 or more, acne, alopecia`},{label:`Rapid virilisation?`,detail:`Clitoromegaly, deep voice, testosterone over 150 ng/dL, DHEAS over 2x ULN -> tumour work-up`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Exclude mimics`,detail:`hCG, TSH, prolactin, morning 17-OHP (over 2 ng/mL -> ACTH test), Cushing screen if suspected`},{label:`Adult or adolescent?`,detail:`Within 8 years of menarche need BOTH irregular cycles and hyperandrogenism - no USG/AMH`,tone:`decision`},{label:`2 of 3 Rotterdam = PCOS`,detail:`PCOM 20 or more follicles 2-9 mm or volume 10 mL or more, or raised AMH in adults`,tone:`good`},{label:`Metabolic and mood screen`,detail:`75 g OGTT, lipids, BP, waist, PHQ-9 - repeat OGTT every 1-3 years`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`PCOS management by her main goal`,root:`Lifestyle and 5-10% weight loss for every woman, then:`,arms:[{label:`Cycle control / endometrium`,tone:`neutral`,steps:[`COC EE 20-30 microgram + LNG daily`,`No COC: MPA 10 mg 12-14 days every 1-3 months`,`LNG-IUS if oestrogen contraindicated`]},{label:`Hirsutism and acne`,tone:`neutral`,steps:[`COC for 6 months first`,`Add spironolactone 25-100 mg with contraception`,`Laser, eflornithine 11.5% cream`]},{label:`Metabolic risk`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Metformin 500 mg -> 1500-2000 mg/day`,`GLP-1 RA for obesity (stop 2 months before conception)`,`OGTT every 1-3 years`]},{label:`Wants pregnancy`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Letrozole 2.5-7.5 mg days 3-7, up to 6 ovulatory cycles`,`Clomiphene plus metformin if letrozole not usable`,`Gonadotrophins (preferred over drilling), then IVF`]}]}]},"gynaecology-vaginal-discharge-pid":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Physiological discharge** is clear or white, non-offensive and varies with the cycle.`,`Pathological discharge changes in colour, odour, amount or consistency, or causes symptoms.`,`Many Indian women report 'white discharge' with backache or stress and no infection.`,`Untreated chlamydia is **asymptomatic in about 70%** and silently damages the tubes.`,`Foul discharge over 35 may be **cervical cancer**; always look at the cervix.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Vaginitis**: bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis, trichomoniasis.`,`**Cervicitis**: chlamydia, gonorrhoea, Mycoplasma genitalium.`,`**Upper-tract infection**: pelvic inflammatory disease (PID).`,`Only cervicitis and PID need gonorrhoea-chlamydia cover and partner treatment.`,`**Amsel criteria (3 of 4)** for BV: thin grey discharge, pH over 4.5, positive whiff, clue cells.`,`**Recurrent candidiasis** is 3 or more symptomatic episodes in a year.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Oestrogen loads vaginal cells with glycogen.`,`2. **Lactobacilli** turn glycogen into lactic acid, keeping pH 3.8-4.5.`,`3. In BV, lactobacilli are lost and Gardnerella and anaerobes form a biofilm (clue cells).`,`4. Anaerobic **amines** smell fishy, especially after sex or menses.`,`5. Menses, IUCD insertion, abortion or delivery breach the cervical barrier.`,`6. Infection ascends to endometrium, tubes and ovaries, causing PID.`,`7. Tubal scarring causes **infertility** and a 6-10 fold ectopic risk.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask colour, consistency and odour, and whether worse after sex or periods.`,`**Itch** points to candida; **fishy odour without itch** points to BV.`,`Pelvic pain, deep dyspareunia, fever or postcoital bleeding point to cervicitis or PID.`,`Ask the LMP; pelvic pain with a missed period is **ectopic** until proved otherwise.`,`Take a private sexual history and ask about recent IUCD insertion or abortion.`,`Ask about diabetes, antibiotics, steroids and douching.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Check temperature; **over 38.3 C** supports PID.`,`On speculum, see whether discharge comes from the **vaginal walls or the os**.`,`**Mucopurulent discharge** and a friable cervix that bleeds on swabbing mean cervicitis.`,`Test pH from the lateral vaginal wall: **over 4.5** suggests BV or trichomonas.`,`Do the whiff test and saline and KOH wet mounts at the bedside.`,`On bimanual, **cervical motion, uterine or adnexal tenderness** suggests PID.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Urine pregnancy test** in every woman with pelvic pain.`,`Bedside pH, whiff test and wet mounts separate the three vaginitides.`,`**NAAT (PCR)** for chlamydia and gonorrhoea is the test of choice where available.`,`Offer HIV, syphilis (RPR or VDRL with TPHA) and HBsAg to every woman with an STI.`,`For PID: CBC, CRP, urine microscopy and TVS if a mass or abscess is suspected.`,`Check glucose and HIV in recurrent candidiasis.`]},{title:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Vaginitis itches or smells with a normal cervix.`,`Cervicitis gives a friable, bleeding os with pus.`,`**PID (CDC minimum criteria)**: pelvic pain plus any one of cervical motion, uterine or adnexal tenderness.`,`One finding is enough to treat PID; waiting costs her tubes.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Where tests are unavailable, use **NACO colour-coded kits**.`,`**Kit 2 (green)** for vaginitis: secnidazole 2 g plus fluconazole 150 mg, single doses.`,`**Kit 1 (grey)** for cervicitis: azithromycin 1 g plus cefixime 400 mg.`,`**BV**: metronidazole 400-500 mg twice daily for 7 days.`,`**Candida**: fluconazole 150 mg single dose or clotrimazole 500 mg pessary.`,`**Trichomonas**: metronidazole 500 mg twice daily for 7 days and treat all partners.`,`**PID outpatient**: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once, then doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Admit PID** if surgical emergency not excluded, abscess, pregnancy, severe illness or 72-hour failure.`,`Inpatient PID: ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily plus doxycycline and metronidazole.`,`Drain a tubo-ovarian abscess over 7 cm or not improving in 48-72 hours.`,`Recurrent candidiasis: fluconazole 150 mg every 72 hours for 3 doses, then weekly for 6 months.`,`Use the **four Cs**: compliance, counselling, condoms and contact treatment.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Pregnancy: use **topical clotrimazole for 7 days**; avoid oral fluconazole.`,`Pregnancy: no doxycycline; use **azithromycin** and admit for PID.`,`IUCD with PID: treat without removing; remove if no response in 48-72 hours.`,`Discharge in a prepubertal girl: think foreign body or **sexual abuse**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for discharge and PID`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Metronidazole`,`400-500 mg oral twice daily`,`7 days (BV, trichomonas); 14 days PID`,`Metallic taste; no alcohol till 24 h after`],[`Secnidazole`,`2 g oral single dose`,`Once (Kit 2)`,`Nausea; no alcohol 72 h`],[`Fluconazole`,`150 mg oral single dose`,`Recurrent: weekly for 6 months`,`Avoid in pregnancy; raises warfarin`],[`Clotrimazole pessary`,`500 mg vaginal once, or 100 mg nightly`,`1 day, or 6-7 days`,`Local burning; 7 days in pregnancy`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`500 mg IM single; 1 g IV daily inpatient`,`Once, or until improving`,`Injection pain, allergy`],[`Cefixime`,`400 mg oral single dose`,`Once`,`Diarrhoea; kit alternative to ceftriaxone`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`7 days chlamydia; 14 days PID`,`Oesophagitis; not in pregnancy or under 8`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g oral single dose`,`Once`,`GI upset, QT; chlamydia choice in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Telling the three vaginitides apart`,columns:[`Feature`,`Bacterial vaginosis`,`Candidiasis`,`Trichomoniasis`],rows:[[`Discharge`,`Thin, grey-white, homogeneous`,`Thick, curdy white`,`Frothy, greenish-yellow`],[`Main symptom`,`Fishy odour, no itch`,`Intense itch, soreness`,`Soreness, dysuria`],[`Vaginal pH`,`Over 4.5`,`4.5 or below`,`Over 4.5`],[`Microscopy`,`Clue cells over 20%`,`Hyphae, budding yeast on KOH`,`Motile trichomonads`],[`Partner treatment`,`Consider only if recurrent`,`No`,`Yes, always`]]},{heading:`NACO syndromic kits for women`,columns:[`Kit`,`Syndrome`,`Contents`],rows:[[`Kit 1 - Grey`,`Cervicitis`,`Azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg`],[`Kit 2 - Green`,`Vaginitis`,`Secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg`],[`Kit 6 - Yellow`,`Lower abdominal pain (PID)`,`Cefixime, then metronidazole + doxycycline 14 days`],[`Kit 5 - Red`,`Herpetic genital ulcer`,`Acyclovir 400 mg TDS 7 days`]]}],remember:[`Candida is the only vaginitis with a **normal pH**.`,`Amsel 3 of 4: thin discharge, pH over 4.5, whiff, clue cells.`,`Trichomonas: always treat the partner; candida and first BV: do not.`,`One tender finding is enough to treat PID.`,`PID: **ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole**, 14 days.`,`Infertility after PID: 12% after one, 25% after two, 50% after three episodes.`,`Pelvic pain with positive UPT is ectopic, never PID.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ACOG Clinical Practice Update (Oct 2025): consider concurrent male-partner therapy (oral metronidazole plus 2% clindamycin cream to penile skin, 7 days) for recurrent symptomatic BV; no routine partner treatment for candidiasis or first-episode BV.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - vaginal discharge and PID`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Pathological discharge = change in **colour, odour, amount or consistency**, or with itch, soreness, dysuria, bleeding or pain; **physiological** discharge is white/clear, non-offensive, cyclical - explain, do not treat.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Vaginitis** (BV, candida, trichomonas) vs **cervicitis** (chlamydia, gonorrhoea, M. genitalium) vs **PID** - only cervicitis and PID need gonorrhoea-chlamydia cover and partner treatment; NACO **colour-coded kits 1-7**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`BV: loss of lactobacilli, douching; candida: **diabetes, antibiotics, pregnancy, COC, HIV**; STI/PID: **age under 25, new/multiple partners, partner with discharge**, IUCD insertion (first 20 days), abortion, delivery, instrumentation.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Oestrogen -> glycogen -> **lactobacilli -> lactic acid pH 3.8-4.5**; loss -> **Gardnerella biofilm (clue cells) -> amines (fishy)**; chlamydia/gonococcus infect **columnar cells** -> ascent past mucus plug -> endometritis -> **salpingitis -> tubal damage (infertility 12/25/50% after 1/2/3 episodes), ectopic**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Colour, consistency, **odour worse after sex/menses (BV)**, **itch (candida)**, pelvic pain, deep dyspareunia, fever, **PCB/IMB**; **LMP - ectopic until excluded**; sexual risk in private; recent instrumentation; diabetes; previous kits; red flags - foul blood-stained discharge over 35 (cancer).`],[`Key examination`,`Temperature (**over 38.3 C supports PID**), abdomen; speculum with water only - **discharge from walls (vaginitis) or os (cervicitis)**; **mucopurulent friable cervix**; bedside **pH from lateral wall**; whiff test; bimanual - **cervical motion, uterine or adnexal tenderness**.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**pH, whiff, saline and KOH wet mount**; Gram stain (Nugent); **NAAT for chlamydia/gonorrhoea**; **UPT in pelvic pain**; HIV, syphilis, HBsAg for every STI; PID: CBC, CRP, urine, **TVS if mass/TOA**; glucose and HIV in recurrent candida.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**BV - Amsel 3 of 4**: thin grey discharge, **pH over 4.5**, positive whiff, **clue cells over 20%**; candida - curdy, pH normal, hyphae; trichomonas - frothy green, pH over 4.5, motile trichomonads; **PID (CDC minimum)**: pelvic pain + **any one of CMT, uterine or adnexal tenderness**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**BV: metronidazole 400-500 mg BD 7 days** (or gel 0.75% 5 days, clindamycin cream 2% 7 days); **candida: fluconazole 150 mg once** or clotrimazole 500 mg pessary; **trich: metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days** (women); NACO **Kit 2 (green): secnidazole 2 g + fluconazole 150 mg**.`],[`Cervicitis & PID`,`**Gonorrhoea: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM** (cefixime 400 mg Kit 1); **chlamydia: doxycycline 100 mg BD 7 days** (azithromycin 1 g in pregnancy); **PID outpatient: ceftriaxone 500 mg IM + doxycycline 100 mg BD + metronidazole 500 mg BD x 14 days** (Kit 6 yellow).`],[`Escalation / second line`,`**Admit PID**: surgical emergency not excluded, **TOA**, pregnancy, severe illness, vomiting, failed oral therapy 72 h -> **ceftriaxone 1 g IV + doxycycline + metronidazole** or clindamycin + gentamicin; **TOA drain if over 7 cm or no response 48-72 h**.`],[`Partner treatment`,`Treat partners for **trichomonas, cervicitis, PID** regardless of symptoms; **not for candida or first-episode BV**; **recurrent symptomatic BV with male partner - consider partner therapy (ACOG 2025)**; abstain until 7 days after both complete treatment.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: topical clotrimazole 7 days (no oral fluconazole), azithromycin for chlamydia, **no doxycycline**, admit PID; **children**: discharge = foreign body or abuse - no speculum; **postmenopausal**: atrophic vaginitis - estriol cream.`],[`Recurrence`,`Recurrent BV (3 or more/year): confirm, **metronidazole gel twice weekly 4-6 months**; recurrent VVC: **fluconazole 150 mg every 72 h x 3 then weekly for 6 months**; check glucose and HIV.`],[`Complications`,`PID: **infertility, ectopic, chronic pelvic pain, TOA, Fitz-Hugh-Curtis perihepatitis**; BV/trich: preterm birth, PID, HIV acquisition.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Positive UPT with pain/shock (**ectopic**), TOA, no improvement at **72 h**, foul blood-stained discharge over 35 or PCB (biopsy cervix), child with discharge (child protection), non-response to two courses.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Review PID at 72 h**; retest chlamydia/gonorrhoea at **3 months**; test of cure in pregnancy; counsel early USG in future pregnancy; IUCD after PID - treat first, insert after 3 months.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Four Cs - Compliance, Counselling, Condoms, Contact treatment**; **Suraksha clinics** (NACO) with free kits; chlamydia screening of young women lowers PID; avoid douching.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**pH over 4.5 = BV or trich**; **clue cells = BV**; strawberry cervix = trich; **one tender sign is enough to treat PID**; do not remove IUCD for PID unless no response 48-72 h.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Bedside work-up of vaginal discharge`,steps:[{label:`Physiological or pathological?`,detail:`White/clear, cyclical, non-irritant = explain only; no antibiotics`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pelvic pain, fever, PCB?`,detail:`UPT first; bimanual for CMT, uterine, adnexal tenderness -> PID pathway`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Speculum: walls or os?`,detail:`Mucopurulent friable cervix = cervicitis (Kit 1 + partner); walls = vaginitis`},{label:`pH from lateral wall`,detail:`4.5 or below = candida/physiological; over 4.5 = BV or trichomonas`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Whiff and wet mounts`,detail:`Clue cells over 20% (BV); hyphae on KOH (candida); motile trichomonads on saline`},{label:`Treat cause, Four Cs, STI panel`,detail:`HIV, syphilis, HBsAg; partner treatment for trich, cervicitis, PID`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`PID - outpatient to inpatient`,steps:[{label:`Suspect PID: pain + one tender sign`,detail:`CMT, uterine or adnexal tenderness; exclude ectopic and appendicitis`},{label:`Outpatient triple therapy 14 days`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM once + doxycycline 100 mg BD + metronidazole 500 mg BD (Kit 6: cefixime 400 mg)`,tone:`good`},{label:`Review at 72 hours`,detail:`No improvement -> re-examine, TVS, admit; do not just repeat the prescription`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Inpatient IV therapy`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily + doxycycline + metronidazole; or clindamycin 900 mg 8-hourly + gentamicin`},{label:`Tubo-ovarian abscess`,detail:`Drain (image-guided or laparoscopic) if over 7 cm or no response in 48-72 h; ruptured TOA = laparotomy`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Vaginitis look-alikes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Bacterial vaginosis`,`Candidiasis`,`Trichomoniasis`],rows:[[`Discharge`,`Thin grey, fishy`,`Thick curdy, itchy`,`Frothy green-yellow`],[`pH`,`Over 4.5`,`4.5 or below`,`Over 4.5`],[`Microscopy`,`Clue cells over 20%`,`Hyphae, budding yeast`,`Motile trichomonads`],[`Treatment`,`Metronidazole 400-500 mg BD 7 days`,`Fluconazole 150 mg once`,`Metronidazole 500 mg BD 7 days`],[`Partner`,`Not routinely; consider if recurrent (ACOG 2025)`,`No`,`Yes, always`]]}]},"gynaecology-infertility-workup":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Infertility is no pregnancy after **12 months** of regular unprotected intercourse.`,`Investigate after **6 months** if the woman is 35 or over.`,`Investigate **at once** with amenorrhoea, known tubal disease, known male factor or age 38 or over.`,`About 85% of couples conceive within 12 months; fecundability is 20-25% per cycle.`,`The woman's **age** is the strongest predictor of success.`,`Infertility is a problem of the **couple**; test both from the first visit.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Primary**: never conceived; **secondary**: a previous conception however it ended.`,`Causes: male 30-40%, ovulatory 25-30%, tubal and peritoneal 25-35%, unexplained 10-15%.`,`**WHO group I**: low FSH, hypothalamic amenorrhoea.`,`**WHO group II**: normal FSH, mostly PCOS (about 85% of anovulation).`,`**WHO group III**: high FSH, premature ovarian insufficiency.`,`Genital TB causes **5-15%** of female infertility in Indian series.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Conception needs an egg, enough sperm, patent tubes and a receptive endometrium.`,`2. After 35, egg number and quality fall, raising aneuploidy and miscarriage.`,`3. Infection or TB destroys tubal cilia and blocks the lumen.`,`4. **Hydrosalpinx** fluid is embryotoxic and roughly halves IVF implantation.`,`5. Spermatogenesis takes about **64-74 days**, so semen reflects the last 3 months.`,`6. **Exogenous testosterone** suppresses FSH and LH and can cause azoospermia.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`See the couple together and say infertility is **not the woman's fault**.`,`Ask duration of trying, the woman's age and coital frequency.`,`A regular 24-38 day cycle is about 95% predictive of ovulation.`,`Ask about post-abortal or puerperal fever, PID, D and C and past TB.`,`Male history: undescended testis, mumps orchitis, erectile function, testosterone or steroids.`,`**Coital history** is the commonest omission: frequency, penetration, vaginismus.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Woman: BMI, thyroid, galactorrhoea, hirsutism and acanthosis.`,`A **fixed retroverted uterus** with uterosacral nodules suggests endometriosis.`,`Man: testicular volume by orchidometer; **under 15 mL** suggests impaired spermatogenesis.`,`Palpate both vasa; an **absent vas** means obstructive azoospermia and CFTR testing.`,`Examine for varicocele standing, with Valsalva.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Semen analysis first**, before any invasive test on the woman.`,`Repeat an abnormal semen result after **3 months**.`,`Confirm ovulation with **mid-luteal progesterone 7 days before the expected period**.`,`Progesterone **over 3 ng/mL (10 nmol/L)** confirms ovulation.`,`**AMH or antral follicle count**, not FSH, predict ovarian response.`,`**HSG on days 6-11** for low-risk women; laparoscopy with dye if high risk.`,`Genital TB: endometrial **CBNAAT, culture and histology**, never Mantoux alone.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Advise intercourse **every 2-3 days** through the cycle.`,`Aim for BMI 19-30; 5-10% weight loss restores ovulation in many obese women.`,`Both partners stop smoking and tobacco; men stop testosterone and steroids.`,`**Folic acid 400 microgram** daily from the first visit (5 mg if high risk).`,`Rubella vaccinate if non-immune, with 1 month of contraception after.`,`**Letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7** is first-line ovulation induction in PCOS.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Clomiphene 50 mg days 2-6, up to 150 mg, maximum 6 ovulatory cycles.`,`Track follicles; cancel if **3 or more follicles over 16 mm**.`,`Hyperprolactinaemia: **cabergoline 0.25 mg twice weekly**, with food at bedtime.`,`Hypothyroidism: levothyroxine to **TSH under 2.5** before conception.`,`Unexplained infertility goes stepwise: expectant, then stimulated IUI, then IVF.`,`Tubal disease goes to IVF; severe male factor goes to **ICSI**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**OHSS**: pain, ascites, breathlessness or oliguria after hCG trigger; admit if severe.`,`Severe OHSS includes clinical ascites, haematocrit over 45% and oliguria.`,`Treated conceptions need an **early scan at 6-7 weeks** for ectopic and multiples.`,`Galactorrhoea with headache or field loss: urgent MRI for macroadenoma.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Complete the first-line work-up within **2 cycles**.`,`Refer at once: age 38 or over, amenorrhoea, low AMH, tubal disease, severe endometriosis.`,`Refer the man with azoospermia, count under 5 million/mL or absent vasa.`,`Refer after 6 ovulatory cycles without pregnancy.`,`ART clinics must be registered under the **ART (Regulation) Act 2021**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in the infertility work-up`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Letrozole`,`2.5 mg oral days 3-7, up to 7.5 mg`,`Up to 6 ovulatory cycles`,`Hot flushes, headache; off-label in India`],[`Clomiphene citrate`,`50 mg oral days 2-6, up to 150 mg`,`Max 6 ovulatory cycles`,`Visual blurring (stop), twins, thin endometrium`],[`hCG trigger`,`5000-10 000 IU IM once`,`When lead follicle is 18-20 mm`,`OHSS; ovulation in about 36 h`],[`Cabergoline`,`0.25 mg oral twice weekly, up to 1 mg`,`Stop once pregnant`,`Nausea, postural hypotension`],[`Levothyroxine`,`25-50 microgram oral daily`,`Titrate 6-weekly`,`Empty stomach; 4 h from iron, calcium`],[`Metformin`,`500 mg oral, up to 1500-2000 mg/day`,`Adjunct only`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency`],[`Folic acid`,`400 microgram oral daily (5 mg high risk)`,`Until 12 weeks of pregnancy`,`None significant`],[`ATT for genital TB`,`2HRZE + 4HRE weight-band FDC`,`6 months`,`Hepatotoxicity; give pyridoxine`]]},{heading:`WHO 2021 semen analysis lower limits`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Lower limit`,`Term if below`],rows:[[`Volume`,`1.4 mL`,`Hypospermia`],[`Concentration`,`16 million/mL`,`Oligozoospermia`],[`Total number`,`39 million per ejaculate`,`Oligozoospermia`],[`Total motility`,`42%`,`Asthenozoospermia`],[`Progressive motility`,`30%`,`Asthenozoospermia`],[`Vitality`,`54% live`,`Necrozoospermia`],[`Normal forms`,`4%`,`Teratozoospermia`]]},{heading:`First-line work-up of the couple`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`Semen analysis`,`Male factor`,`First, 2-7 days abstinence`],[`Mid-luteal progesterone`,`Ovulation`,`7 days before expected period`],[`AMH or antral follicle count`,`Ovarian reserve and response`,`Any day (AFC day 2-5)`],[`TSH, prolactin`,`Thyroid, prolactin causes`,`If symptoms or irregular cycles`],[`Transvaginal ultrasound`,`Fibroids, PCOM, hydrosalpinx`,`At first work-up`],[`HSG`,`Tubal patency`,`Days 6-11, low-risk women`],[`Laparoscopy and dye`,`Tubes, adhesions, endometriosis`,`High-risk women`]]}],remember:[`12 months, **6 if 35 or over**, at once with amenorrhoea or tubal disease.`,`**Semen before HSG**; the reverse is the classic sequencing error.`,`WHO 2021: 1.4 mL, 16 million/mL, 39 million, 42%, 30%, 4%.`,`Progesterone 7 days before the period, not always day 21.`,`AMH predicts response to stimulation, not natural fertility.`,`Never start ATT on a positive Mantoux alone.`,`Testosterone in a man trying to conceive causes azoospermia.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`NICE NG257 (Mar 2026) replaced CG156: use AMH or antral follicle count, not FSH, to predict ovarian response; tags moved to NICE NG257 2026.`,`WHO first infertility guideline (Nov 2025): unexplained infertility managed stepwise - expectant management, then stimulated IUI with clomiphene or letrozole (not gonadotrophins), then IVF; NICE: 2 years of trying, no stand-alone ovarian stimulation.`,`WHO 2025: letrozole alone preferred over letrozole plus metformin; clomiphene plus metformin where letrozole cannot be used; gonadotrophins preferred over ovarian drilling.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - infertility first-line work-up`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`No clinical pregnancy after **12 months of regular (every 2-3 days) unprotected intercourse**; investigate after **6 months if woman 35 or over**, **at once** with amenorrhoea, past PID/surgery, stage III-IV endometriosis, known male factor or age 38+. 85% conceive in 12 months; fecundability 20-25%/cycle.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Causes**: male **30-40%**, ovulatory **25-30%** (WHO group I hypogonadotropic, **II normogonadotropic - PCOS 85%**, III hypergonadotropic/POI), tubal-peritoneal **25-35%**, uterine 5-10%, **unexplained 10-15%**; both partners in 20-30%.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Woman's age (most important)**, PID/chlamydia, **genital TB (5-15% in India)**, PCOS, endometriosis, smoking/tobacco, obesity, **testosterone or anabolic steroids in men**, mumps orchitis, varicocele, chemotherapy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pulsatile GnRH -> FSH/LH -> follicle -> **LH surge -> ovulation 34-36 h** -> corpus luteum progesterone 14 days; **AMH mirrors follicle pool (predicts response, not natural conception)**; hydrosalpinx fluid halves IVF implantation; exogenous testosterone -> low FSH/LH -> **azoospermia**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**See the couple together**; duration, coital frequency, dyspareunia, lubricants; cycle regularity (regular 24-38 days = 95% ovulatory), **hypomenorrhoea (TB, Asherman)**, PID, surgery; man - mumps, testosterone use, erectile/ejaculatory problems; smoking, alcohol, rubella status.`],[`Key examination`,`Woman: BMI, hirsutism, acanthosis, galactorrhoea, thyroid, pelvic tenderness, fixed retroversion, nodules; man: **testicular volume (orchidometer)**, varicocele, **vasa palpable**, secondary sex characters.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Semen analysis first** (WHO 2021: volume 1.4 mL, **16 million/mL**, progressive motility 30%, morphology 4%; repeat abnormal after 3 months); **mid-luteal progesterone over 3 ng/mL (10 nmol/L)**; TSH, prolactin if indicated; **AMH or AFC (not FSH) for reserve**; TVS; **HSG day 6-11** (laparoscopy if high risk).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Unexplained infertility (WHO 2025)**: 12 months, normal history/exam both partners, ovulation confirmed, **tubes patent, semen within WHO limits**; azoospermia on two samples - FSH, karyotype, Y-microdeletion; **genital TB - premenstrual endometrial CBNAAT, culture, histology** (never Mantoux).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Lifestyle: **BMI 19-30**, stop tobacco both, folic acid, intercourse every 2-3 days; **anovulatory PCOS: letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7 (to 7.5 mg)**, max 6 ovulatory cycles with follicle tracking; **hyperprolactinaemia: cabergoline**; hypothyroid: levothyroxine to TSH under 2.5.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`Clomiphene 50-150 mg days 2-6 (**with metformin if letrozole not usable**); **gonadotrophins 37.5-75 IU step-up (preferred over drilling)**; **hCG trigger 5000-10 000 IU** at follicle 18-20 mm; **unexplained: expectant -> stimulated IUI -> IVF**; tubal disease -> IVF (salpingectomy for hydrosalpinx); **ICSI** severe male factor.`],[`Male factor`,`Never testosterone; **hypogonadotropic hypogonadism: hCG 1500-2000 IU twice weekly +/- FSH**; refer concentration under 5 million/mL or azoospermia for genetics and ICSI planning; varicocele repair selected cases.`],[`Special groups`,`Woman **38 or over, very low AMH** - refer at once; **genital TB** - standard 2HRZE/4HRE, tubal surgery futile; PCOS - highest OHSS risk; India - letrozole off-label (consent), **ART Act 2021** regulates clinics.`],[`Complications`,`**OHSS** (ascites, Hct over 45%, oliguria - admit), **multiple pregnancy, ectopic** (early USG at 6-7 weeks), psychological distress, cost.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Refer at once: 38+, amenorrhoea, **raised FSH/very low AMH**, tubal disease, Asherman, stage III-IV endometriosis, azoospermia; **after 6 ovulatory cycles** without pregnancy; urgent - OHSS, macroadenoma, ectopic.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Complete the work-up **within 2 cycles** and review results together; each induction cycle - follicle tracking, cancel if **3 or more follicles over 16 mm**; mid-luteal progesterone.`],[`Do not do`,`No BBT charts, post-coital test, antisperm antibodies, **Mantoux/IGRA to diagnose genital TB**, empirical ATT, progesterone for 'luteal defect', **FSH to predict ovarian response**, stand-alone clomiphene/letrozole for unexplained infertility.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO infertility guideline (Nov 2025)** - first global guideline, 40 recommendations; **NICE NG257 (Mar 2026)** replaced CG156 - **AMH/AFC not FSH**, unexplained infertility 2 years before treatment, no stand-alone ovarian stimulation.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Semen analysis is the first test**; day 21 progesterone over 3 ng/mL = ovulation; **AMH predicts response, not natural fertility**; **letrozole is first-line in PCOS**; never treat genital TB on Mantoux alone.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Infertility work-up within two cycles`,steps:[{label:`Couple together: when to start?`,detail:`12 months; 6 months if 35 or over; at once if amenorrhoea, PID, endometriosis III-IV, male factor, 38+`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Semen analysis (WHO 2021)`,detail:`16 million/mL, progressive motility 30%, morphology 4%; repeat abnormal after 3 months`,tone:`good`},{label:`Confirm ovulation`,detail:`Mid-luteal progesterone 7 days before period; over 3 ng/mL confirms; irregular cycles need no proof`},{label:`TSH, prolactin, AMH or AFC`,detail:`AMH/AFC predict ovarian response - not FSH (NICE NG257 2026)`},{label:`Tubal patency`,detail:`HSG day 6-11 if low risk; laparoscopy + dye if PID, ectopic, surgery or endometriosis`,tone:`decision`},{label:`All normal = unexplained`,detail:`Expectant -> stimulated IUI (CC/letrozole) -> IVF (WHO 2025)`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Ovulation induction ladder (WHO group II / PCOS)`,steps:[{label:`Weight loss 5-10%, folic acid`,detail:`Restores ovulation in many obese anovulatory women`,tone:`good`},{label:`Letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7`,detail:`Increase by 2.5 mg to 7.5 mg; follicle tracking; up to 6 ovulatory cycles; letrozole alone, not with metformin`},{label:`Clomiphene 50-150 mg days 2-6 + metformin`,detail:`Where letrozole cannot be used (WHO 2025); stop if visual symptoms`},{label:`Gonadotrophins low-dose step-up`,detail:`rFSH or hMG 37.5-75 IU SC daily; preferred over ovarian drilling; cancel if 3+ follicles over 16 mm`,tone:`warn`},{label:`IVF`,detail:`Antagonist protocol with agonist trigger to prevent OHSS`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Unexplained infertility - WHO 2025 vs NICE NG257 2026`,columns:[`Point`,`WHO 2025`,`NICE NG257 2026`],rows:[[`Diagnosis`,`12 months, ovulation and patent tubes confirmed, normal semen`,`Same work-up`],[`First step`,`Expectant management`,`Keep trying - total of 2 years before treatment`],[`Ovarian stimulation`,`Only with IUI (clomiphene or letrozole, not gonadotrophins)`,`No stand-alone ovarian stimulation`],[`Next`,`Stimulated IUI, then IVF`,`Treatment individualised by age, ovarian reserve and male factor`]]}]},"gynaecology-dysmenorrhoea-endometriosis-fibroids":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Primary dysmenorrhoea** is period pain with no pelvic pathology, starting 6-24 months after menarche.`,`It affects 50-90% of adolescents and young women.`,`**Secondary dysmenorrhoea** has a cause: endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, chronic PID.`,`**Endometriosis** is endometrial-like tissue outside the uterus, in about 10% of women.`,`**Adenomyosis** is endometrial tissue within the myometrium.`,`**Fibroids** are benign smooth-muscle tumours, clinically evident in 20-40% of women.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Endometriosis types: superficial peritoneal, ovarian endometrioma, deep (over 5 mm).`,`**Revised ASRM stages I-IV** predict fertility, not pain.`,`Fibroids use **FIGO types 0-8** by position.`,`Only **types 0-2 (submucosal)** reliably cause heavy bleeding and infertility.`,`Suspect secondary pain with onset after 25, progressive pain or failed NSAID plus COC.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Progesterone withdrawal releases arachidonic acid in the endometrium.`,`2. COX converts it to **PGF2-alpha**, causing strong dysrhythmic contractions.`,`3. Contractions occlude vessels, giving ischaemic 'uterine angina'.`,`4. NSAIDs block COX, so they remove the cause if started early.`,`5. Endometriosis follows **Sampson's retrograde menstruation**, with immune and genetic factors.`,`6. Lesions make their own oestrogen (aromatase) and resist progesterone.`,`7. Bleeding deposits cause inflammation, fibrosis and adhesions that fix the uterus.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Primary pain starts with the flow and lasts **8-72 hours**.`,`Secondary pain starts days before and continues after the period.`,`Ask about **deep dyspareunia**, dyschezia and cyclical rectal bleeding or haematuria.`,`Heavy regular periods with clots suggest fibroids or adenomyosis.`,`Pressure symptoms: frequency, retention, constipation, leg swelling.`,`Ask her **fertility plans** before choosing any treatment.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`In virgins, examine only the abdomen and vulva.`,`**Endometriosis**: fixed retroverted uterus, tender nodular uterosacral ligaments.`,`**Adenomyosis**: symmetrically bulky, globular, soft, tender uterus.`,`**Fibroid**: firm, irregular, non-tender mass that moves with the cervix.`,`Cyclical pain with primary amenorrhoea: look for a **bulging bluish membrane** (imperforate hymen).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Typical primary dysmenorrhoea with a normal examination needs no tests.`,`Pregnancy test and chlamydia-gonorrhoea NAAT if sexually active.`,`**Transvaginal ultrasound** first: fibroid FIGO type, adenomyosis, ground-glass endometrioma.`,`A normal scan **does not exclude** peritoneal endometriosis.`,`MRI for deep endometriosis or a junctional zone of **12 mm or more** (adenomyosis).`,`**Do not use CA-125** to diagnose or exclude endometriosis.`,`Laparoscopy is no longer needed before treatment.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Local heat and exercise help; heat at 40 C matches an NSAID.`,`**NSAID 1-2 days before** the period, taken regularly for 2-3 days.`,`Try at least 3 cycles and another NSAID before calling it a failure.`,`Add a **COC**, continuous for endometriosis, or an LNG-IUS.`,`**Dienogest 2 mg daily** continuously is first-line for endometriosis pain.`,`**LNG-IUS** is first-line for adenomyosis.`,`Fibroid bleeding: tranexamic acid and mefenamic acid; these do not shrink fibroids.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**GnRH agonist** with add-back from the start, limited to 6 months.`,`Bone density falls about 6% in 6 months without add-back.`,`Endometrioma: **excise the capsule** rather than drain, though it lowers AMH.`,`Endometriosis with infertility: surgery, stimulated IUI, then IVF; hormones do not help fertility.`,`Fibroids by FIGO type: **hysteroscopic resection for types 0-2**, myomectomy, or hysterectomy.`,`Uterine artery embolisation shrinks fibroids 40-60%; not preferred if pregnancy is wanted.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`A fibroid growing after menopause suggests **leiomyosarcoma**; refer and avoid morcellation.`,`Red degeneration in mid-pregnancy: pain and fever; analgesia, no surgery.`,`Sudden pain with vomiting and a tender mass suggests torsion; same-hour referral.`,`Hydronephrosis or urinary retention from a fibroid needs urgent referral.`,`Fibroids with cavity distortion are **WHO MEC 4** for IUDs.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review primary dysmenorrhoea after **3 cycles**; failure means scan and rethink.`,`Review endometriosis on hormones at 3-6 months; recurrence is up to 50% at 5 years.`,`Asymptomatic fibroids need no treatment; check Hb and review yearly.`,`Refer deep endometriosis, endometrioma, infertility and failed medical treatment.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for dysmenorrhoea, endometriosis and fibroids`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mefenamic acid`,`500 mg oral three times daily with food`,`2-3 days, start 1-2 days early`,`Dyspepsia, GI bleed, renal impairment`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg oral three times daily`,`2-3 days per period`,`Dyspepsia; avoid in peptic ulcer`],[`Naproxen`,`500 mg, then 250 mg every 6-8 hours`,`2-3 days per period`,`Dyspepsia, fluid retention`],[`COC (EE 30 + LNG 150)`,`1 tablet oral daily`,`Cyclic or continuous`,`VTE, spotting; check WHO MEC`],[`Dienogest`,`2 mg oral daily`,`Continuous`,`Irregular bleeding, low mood, acne`],[`LNG-IUS 52 mg`,`Intrauterine`,`Up to 5 years`,`Spotting 3-6 months; not if cavity distorted`],[`Leuprolide`,`3.75 mg IM monthly or 11.25 mg 3-monthly`,`Up to 6 months`,`Hot flushes, bone loss; add-back tibolone 2.5 mg`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g oral three times daily`,`Up to 4 days per period`,`Avoid in active VTE`]]},{heading:`Causes of painful periods compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Primary`,`Endometriosis`,`Adenomyosis`,`Fibroid`],rows:[[`Typical woman`,`Adolescent`,`25-35, infertility`,`Parous, 35-50`,`30-45`],[`Pain pattern`,`With flow, 8-72 hours`,`Before and after flow, dyspareunia`,`Heavy painful periods`,`Bleeding; pain if degenerating`],[`Pelvis`,`Normal`,`Fixed retroverted, nodular uterosacrals`,`Bulky, globular, tender`,`Firm, irregular, non-tender`],[`Imaging`,`None`,`Ground-glass endometrioma`,`Junctional zone 12 mm or more`,`TVS with FIGO type`],[`First treatment`,`NSAID early, then COC`,`NSAID + COC or dienogest`,`LNG-IUS`,`Tranexamic acid, NSAID`]]},{heading:`FIGO fibroid types and approach`,columns:[`Type`,`Location`,`Usual approach`],rows:[[`0`,`Pedunculated intracavitary`,`Hysteroscopic resection`],[`1`,`Submucosal, under 50% intramural`,`Hysteroscopic resection`],[`2`,`Submucosal, 50% or more intramural`,`Hysteroscopic or abdominal`],[`3-4`,`Intramural`,`Myomectomy if symptomatic`],[`5-7`,`Subserosal, 7 is pedunculated`,`Laparoscopic myomectomy; 7 can twist`],[`8`,`Cervical, broad ligament, parasitic`,`Individualised`]]}],remember:[`NSAID **before** the pain, regularly; 'as needed' is the commonest failure.`,`Pain before and after the flow with deep dyspareunia is secondary.`,`Treat endometriosis empirically; no laparoscopy needed first.`,`**CA-125 never diagnoses endometriosis**.`,`Hormone suppression does not treat endometriosis infertility.`,`GnRH agonist: 6 months maximum, with add-back.`,`Only FIGO types 0-2 fibroids reliably cause HMB.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - dysmenorrhoea, endometriosis and fibroids`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Primary dysmenorrhoea**: cyclical pain with normal pelvis, starts **6-24 months after menarche**; **secondary**: due to pathology (endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, PID, IUCD, obstruction). **Endometriosis** = endometrial-like tissue outside uterus (**10%**); **adenomyosis** = within myometrium; **fibroid** = monoclonal smooth-muscle tumour.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**rASRM stage I-IV** (1-5, 6-15, 16-40, over 40 points - predicts fertility, not pain), #Enzian for deep disease; **FIGO leiomyoma types 0-8** - **0-2 submucosal** (bleeding, infertility, hysteroscopic), 3-5 intramural, 6-7 subserosal, 8 other.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Endometriosis: **Sampson retrograde menstruation**, coelomic metaplasia, spread; family history 6-7 fold, nulliparity; adenomyosis: parous 35-50, curettage; fibroids: **MED12**, early menarche, nulliparity, obesity, African/Indian ethnicity.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Progesterone withdrawal -> arachidonic acid -> **PGF2-alpha** -> myometrial ischaemic contractions -> pain (**NSAIDs remove the cause**); endometriosis: **aromatase + progesterone resistance** -> inflammation -> neuroangiogenesis -> adhesions; fibroids grow on **oestrogen and progesterone**.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Timing - primary **with flow, 8-72 h**; secondary **before and after flow, worsening**; **deep dyspareunia, dyschezia, cyclical haematuria/rectal bleeding** (deep endometriosis); heavy periods, pressure symptoms; fertility plans; adolescent with **primary amenorrhoea + cyclical pain = obstruction**.`],[`Key examination`,`Adolescent: abdomen and vulva only; endometriosis - **fixed retroverted uterus, nodular tender uterosacrals**, bluish posterior fornix nodules; adenomyosis - **globular soft tender uterus under 12-14 weeks**; fibroid - **firm irregular non-tender, moves with cervix**.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**None for typical primary dysmenorrhoea**; UPT, NAAT if infection possible; **TVS first** (endometrioma ground-glass, FIGO type); **MRI** - junctional zone **12 mm or more** (adenomyosis), deep endometriosis map; Hb, ferritin; **CA-125 not for diagnosis**; laparoscopy only if imaging negative and treatment fails.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Endometriosis is a **clinical/imaging diagnosis - treat without laparoscopy**; histology remains reference; adenomyosis on TVS/MRI; fibroid type on TVS or hysteroscopy.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Primary: **heat, NSAID started 1-2 days before** (mefenamic acid 500 mg TDS, naproxen 500 then 250 mg 6-8 h) for 3 cycles -> add **COC (continuous)** or LNG-IUS; endometriosis: **NSAID + continuous COC or dienogest 2 mg daily**; adenomyosis: **LNG-IUS**; fibroid HMB: **tranexamic acid 1 g TDS** + NSAID, LNG-IUS if cavity normal.`],[`Escalation / second line`,`**GnRH agonist** - leuprolide 3.75 mg monthly or goserelin 3.6 mg, **with add-back from the start** (tibolone 2.5 mg or E2 1 mg + NETA 0.5 mg); **relugolix combination** (40 mg + E2 1 mg + NETA 0.5 mg); letrozole for refractory pain (specialist); ulipristal only if not eligible for surgery; no danazol.`],[`Surgery / procedures`,`Endometriosis: **laparoscopic excision/ablation**, **endometrioma cystectomy (not drainage - recurs over 80%), but lowers AMH**; fibroids: **hysteroscopic myomectomy types 0-2**, laparoscopic/open myomectomy, **UAE (40-60% shrinkage)**, hysterectomy; **contained morcellation**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Infertility with endometriosis**: surgery for mild, **stimulated IUI**, IVF for moderate-severe; **fibroid in pregnancy**: red degeneration - **paracetamol, rest, no NSAID after 20-30 weeks**; avoid myomectomy at caesarean; adolescents - rule out obstructed Mullerian anomaly.`],[`Complications`,`Endometriosis: infertility, endometrioma, **bowel/ureteric obstruction**, recurrence **50% at 5 years**; fibroids: anaemia, retention, hydronephrosis, degeneration, torsion (pedunculated), **sarcoma rare (growth after menopause)**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Urgent: **postmenopausal or rapidly growing fibroid**, mass with ascites/raised CA-125 after menopause, hydronephrosis, retention, **torsion**; refer deep endometriosis, endometrioma, infertility, cavity distortion, uncontrolled anaemia.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Primary: review after **3 cycles**; endometriosis on hormones **3-6 monthly**; fibroids - Hb and symptoms yearly; GnRH over 6 months - **DXA**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**NSAID before the pain**; **hormones cannot improve fertility**; **CA-125 does not diagnose endometriosis**; **LNG-IUS first-line for adenomyosis**; fibroids without cavity distortion are **MEC 1 for IUDs, with distortion 4**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Painful periods - primary or secondary?`,steps:[{label:`Onset and timing`,detail:`Within 6-24 months of menarche, pain with flow 8-72 h = primary`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Red-flag features?`,detail:`Onset after 25, worsening, dyspareunia, dyschezia, HMB, IMB, infertility, amenorrhoea with cyclical pain`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Primary: treat empirically`,detail:`Heat + NSAID 1-2 days before onset for 3 cycles, then add COC or LNG-IUS`,tone:`good`},{label:`Fails 3-6 months or secondary features`,detail:`UPT, NAAT, TVS (endometrioma, fibroid type, adenomyosis)`},{label:`MRI if TVS inconclusive`,detail:`JZ 12 mm or more = adenomyosis; map deep endometriosis`},{label:`Laparoscopy only if needed`,detail:`Imaging negative and empirical treatment fails, or surgery planned`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Fibroid management ladder`,steps:[{label:`Asymptomatic: observe`,detail:`Hb and review yearly; regress after menopause; growth after menopause = refer`,tone:`good`},{label:`Tranexamic acid + NSAID`,detail:`TXA 1 g TDS up to 4 days, mefenamic acid 500 mg TDS - reduce loss, do not shrink`},{label:`LNG-IUS if cavity not distorted`,detail:`Types 3-7 with normal cavity; not with submucosal fibroids`},{label:`GnRH agonist or relugolix combination`,detail:`Leuprolide 3.75 mg monthly 3 months preoperatively shrinks 35-60%; add-back`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Procedure by FIGO type`,detail:`Types 0-2 hysteroscopic; 3-6 myomectomy; UAE if fertility not wanted`},{label:`Hysterectomy`,detail:`Family complete, failed other options`}]}]},"gynaecology-cervical-cancer-screening":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Cervical cancer is caused by **persistent high-risk HPV** infection.`,`Its **10-20 year precancerous phase** is what screening exploits.`,`It is the **second commonest cancer** in Indian women, about 77 000 deaths a year.`,`**Screening** is for asymptomatic women; a symptomatic woman needs examination and biopsy.`,`WHO **90-70-90** targets for 2030: vaccinate, screen, treat.`,`Elimination means incidence under 4 per 100 000 women-years.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**CIN1 (LSIL)** involves the lower third and usually regresses.`,`**CIN2 and CIN3 (HSIL)** are the treatment threshold.`,`Cytology uses **Bethesda 2014**: NILM, ASC-US, ASC-H, LSIL, HSIL, AGC.`,`Transformation zone **type 1** is fully visible; **type 3** is not fully visible.`,`Invasive cancer is staged by **FIGO 2018**, which allows imaging and pathology.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. HPV enters basal cells of the **transformation zone** through micro-abrasions.`,`2. **80-90% of infections clear** within 2 years.`,`3. In persistent infection, viral DNA integrates into the host genome.`,`4. **E6 degrades p53** and **E7 inactivates Rb**, so damaged cells keep dividing.`,`5. CIN2-3 develops and progresses to invasion over 10-20 years.`,`6. HPV 16 and 18 cause **over 80%** of cancers in India.`,`7. Smoking, HIV, high parity and long COC use aid persistence.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask every woman 30 or over when she was last screened and the result.`,`**Postcoital bleeding**, intermenstrual or postmenopausal bleeding means diagnosis, not screening.`,`Backache, leg swelling or urinary symptoms suggest advanced disease.`,`Note HIV status, past treatment, vaccination and hysterectomy type.`,`Address barriers: fear, embarrassment, need for a female provider.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Use a speculum warmed with water only; **no gel**, which spoils cytology and HPV tests.`,`**VIA**: apply 5% acetic acid, wait **1 minute**, inspect the transformation zone.`,`**VIA positive**: dense, opaque, well-defined acetowhite area **touching the SCJ**.`,`A cauliflower growth or ulcer is **suspicious for cancer**; biopsy, do not screen.`,`**VIA is invalid after menopause** because the SCJ recedes into the canal.`,`HPV self-sampling raises uptake in women who decline a speculum.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**HPV DNA** is the best primary test (sensitivity 90-95%); NPV over 99%.`,`VIA sensitivity is 60-70%; single cytology is 50-60%.`,`An HPV-positive woman is triaged by VIA, cytology or colposcopy.`,`Colposcopy notes transformation zone type and takes a **directed punch biopsy**.`,`Do not HPV-test under 30 or screen with cytology under 25.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Offer **same-visit screen-and-treat**; 40-60% do not return in multi-visit pathways.`,`**Thermal ablation** at 100-120 C for 20-40 seconds per application, no anaesthesia.`,`Ablation needs a **type 1 zone**, lesion under 75% of the ectocervix, no invasion suspicion.`,`Cryotherapy uses a double freeze-thaw: 3 minutes freeze, 5 thaw, 3 freeze.`,`Counsel that HPV may be years old; partner testing is not indicated.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**LLETZ** for lesions not fit for ablation: type 2-3 zone, large, glandular or recurrent.`,`Cold knife cone for suspected microinvasion or adenocarcinoma in situ.`,`**Hysterectomy is never the primary treatment for CIN**.`,`After treatment, avoid intercourse and tampons for 4 weeks.`,`Deep or repeat LLETZ raises preterm birth risk.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Women living with HIV: HPV test **from 25, every 3-5 years**, always triaged.`,`Pregnancy: screening is safe; defer CIN treatment to 6 weeks postpartum.`,`Stop at 65 after two negatives, or after total hysterectomy for benign disease.`,`Continue screening after **subtotal hysterectomy**.`,`Vaccinated women still need screening.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**India**: VIA every 5 years for women **30-65** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs.`,`**WHO**: HPV DNA from 30, every 5-10 years.`,`National HPV programme launched **28 February 2026**: single dose of Gardasil-4 for girls aged 14.`,`WHO 2022: girls 9-14 need **1 or 2 doses**; immunocompromised need at least 2, preferably 3.`,`Give 0.5 mL IM deltoid and observe 15 minutes for syncope.`,`Test of cure with HPV test at **12 months** after treatment.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Vaccines and agents in cervical screening`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Bivalent HPV vaccine (16, 18)`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`9-14 y: 1-2 doses; 15 and over: 3`,`Injection pain, fever, syncope`],[`Quadrivalent HPV vaccine (incl. Cervavac)`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Programme: single dose at 14`,`Syncope; yeast allergy caution`],[`Nonavalent HPV vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`9-14 y: 2 doses; HIV: 3 doses`,`As above; defer in pregnancy`],[`Acetic acid 5%`,`Applied to cervix`,`Read at 1 minute`,`Mild stinging; prepare fresh`],[`Lugol iodine`,`Applied to cervix (VILI)`,`Read at once`,`Avoid in iodine allergy`],[`Lidocaine 2% with adrenaline`,`Cervical block 5-10 mL`,`Before LLETZ`,`Palpitations, tremor`]]},{heading:`Screening tests compared`,columns:[`Test`,`Sensitivity for CIN2+`,`Interval`,`Main limitation`],rows:[[`VIA`,`60-70%`,`5 years (India)`,`Subjective; invalid after menopause`],[`Cytology (Pap)`,`50-60% single test`,`3 years`,`Needs laboratory and cytologists`],[`HPV DNA`,`90-95%`,`5-10 years`,`Cost; positives need triage; not under 30`]]},{heading:`Bethesda 2014 and the next step`,columns:[`Category`,`Meaning`,`Next step`],rows:[[`NILM`,`Negative`,`Routine interval`],[`ASC-US`,`Atypical squamous cells, unclear`,`Reflex HPV; colposcopy if positive`],[`ASC-H`,`Cannot exclude HSIL`,`Colposcopy`],[`LSIL`,`CIN1, HPV effect`,`Colposcopy or HPV follow-up`],[`HSIL`,`CIN2-3`,`Colposcopy or see-and-treat LLETZ`],[`AGC`,`Atypical glandular cells`,`Colposcopy; endometrial sampling if 35 or over`]]}],remember:[`WHO **90-70-90**: vaccinate 90% by 15, screen 70% by 35 and 45, treat 90%.`,`VIA positive = dense acetowhite **touching the SCJ**.`,`Postcoital bleeding or a visible lesion: **biopsy, do not screen**.`,`HPV test NPV over 99% buys the 5-10 year interval.`,`Ablation needs type 1 zone, under 75% of ectocervix, no invasion; else LLETZ.`,`Vaccine is prophylactic, never therapeutic, and never cancels screening.`,`HIV: screen from 25, every 3-5 years.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`India launched the nationwide HPV vaccination programme on 28 February 2026: a single free dose of quadrivalent vaccine (Gardasil-4) for girls aged 14 years, campaign then routine immunisation days.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - cervical cancer screening and HPV vaccination`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Screening = testing **asymptomatic** women to detect **CIN2+** before invasion; a **symptomatic woman (PCB, visible lesion) needs biopsy, not screening**. India: **2nd commonest cancer in women**, about 1.2 lakh cases and 77 000 deaths a year.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**CIN1 (LSIL)** regresses; **CIN2-3 (HSIL)** = treatment threshold; AIS glandular; cytology - **Bethesda 2014** (NILM, ASC-US, ASC-H, LSIL, HSIL, AGC); **transformation zone types 1-3**; FIGO 2018 staging for cancer.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Persistent high-risk HPV (16, 18 = 70% worldwide, over 80% India)**; cofactors - **smoking, HIV (6-fold)**, high parity, long COC use, early sexual debut, multiple partners, other STIs.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Micro-abrasion -> HPV in basal cells of **transformation zone** -> 80-90% clear in 2 years -> **persistence -> integration -> E6 degrades p53, E7 inactivates Rb** -> CIN2-3 -> invasion over **10-20 years** (the screening window).`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Last screen, test and result in every woman 30+**; PCB, IMB, PMB, **foul blood-stained discharge**; backache, leg swelling (advanced); HIV, immunosuppression; previous treatment; hysterectomy type; vaccination status; barriers (female provider).`],[`Key examination`,`Cusco with **water only (no gel)**; **VIA: 5% acetic acid, read at 1 minute - dense opaque acetowhite area touching SCJ = positive**; VILI with Lugol (mustard-yellow abnormal); **VIA invalid if SCJ not visible (postmenopause)**; bimanual/PR for parametrium if cancer suspected.`],[`Screening tests`,`**HPV DNA** (sensitivity 90-95%, NPV over 99%, self-sampling, every 5-10 years); **VIA** (60-70%, immediate, screen-and-treat); **cytology** (50-60%, every 3 years). India **NP-NCD: VIA every 5 years, 30-65 years** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs.`],[`Who and when`,`WHO: **HPV DNA from 30, every 5-10 years**; **women living with HIV from 25, every 3-5 years, always triaged**; **stop at 65 after two negatives**, and after total hysterectomy for benign disease; **no HPV test under 30, no cytology under 25**.`],[`Treatment of precancer`,`**Ablation** if **type 1 TZ, lesion under 75%, no canal extension, no suspicion of invasion**: **thermal ablation 100-120 C, 20-40 s** (preferred) or **cryotherapy 3-5-3 min double freeze**; else **LLETZ** under cervical block; **cold knife cone** for AIS/microinvasion; **never hysterectomy first**.`],[`Vaccination`,`Bivalent, **quadrivalent (incl. Cervavac 2022)**, nonavalent; **WHO 2022: 9-14 and 15-20 years 1 or 2 doses**, 21+ two doses, **HIV/immunocompromised at least 2, ideally 3 doses**; **0.5 mL IM deltoid, observe 15 min (syncope)**; defer in pregnancy, breastfeeding OK.`],[`National HPV programme`,`**Launched 28 Feb 2026**: **single dose Gardasil-4 free for girls aged 14 years** (about 1.15 crore/year), 3-month campaign then routine immunisation days at government facilities, parental consent; private catch-up to 26, 27-45 by shared decision.`],[`Special groups`,`**HIV**: screen from 25, every 3-5 years, triage, ART; **pregnancy**: screening and colposcopy safe, **no ECC**, treat CIN 6 weeks postpartum; **postmenopause**: VIA invalid - HPV or cytology; subtotal hysterectomy - continue screening.`],[`Complications`,`Post-ablation/LLETZ watery discharge 2-4 weeks, bleeding, infection; **LLETZ raises preterm birth risk**; untreated - invasive cancer, ureteric obstruction, fistula.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Visible growth, contact bleeding, suspicious VIA, biopsy-proven cancer, hydronephrosis, leg oedema** - gynae-oncology urgently; colposcopy for VIA-positive not ablation-eligible, HSIL, AGC, HPV-positive with type 3 TZ.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Test of cure at 12 months** (HPV, VIA or cytology); trace every untreated screen-positive via ASHA and register; indicators - coverage, **VIA positivity 5-10%**, 90% treated.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**WHO 90-70-90 by 2030**: 90% girls vaccinated by 15, **70% screened by 35 and 45 with a high-performance test**, 90% treated; tobacco cessation, condoms; ASHA CBAC enumeration.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**India national HPV vaccination programme (Feb 2026)** - single dose Gardasil-4 at 14 years; WHO 2021 screening guideline (HPV DNA primary) still current.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Vaccination never cancels screening**; **VIA invalid after menopause**; HPV-positive partner needs no testing; **biopsy a visible lesion, never smear it**; thermal ablation 100-120 C.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Screen-and-treat visit (VIA or HPV)`,steps:[{label:`Woman 30-65 (25+ if HIV)`,detail:`Symptomatic or visible lesion? -> biopsy and refer, not screening`,tone:`decision`},{label:`HPV DNA if available, else VIA`,detail:`HPV every 5-10 years (self-sample OK); VIA every 5 years; VIA invalid if SCJ not seen`},{label:`VIA: acetic acid 5%, read at 1 min`,detail:`Dense opaque acetowhite touching SCJ = positive; HPV-positive -> VIA/cytology/colposcopy triage`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Eligible for ablation?`,detail:`Type 1 TZ, under 75% of ectocervix, no canal extension, no suspicion of cancer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Same-visit thermal ablation`,detail:`100-120 C, 20-40 s per application; or cryotherapy 3-5-3 min`,tone:`good`},{label:`Not eligible -> LLETZ / colposcopy`,detail:`Type 2-3 zone, large lesion, glandular disease, suspected invasion`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Test of cure at 12 months`,detail:`HPV, VIA or cytology; positive -> repeat colposcopy`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`HPV vaccination - programme vs WHO schedules`,columns:[`Group`,`Schedule`,`Note`],rows:[[`India national programme (Feb 2026)`,`Single dose Gardasil-4 at 14 years, free`,`Government facilities; parental consent`],[`Girls 9-14 (WHO 2022)`,`1 or 2 doses (2 doses 0, 6 months as licensed)`,`Before sexual debut`],[`15-20 years`,`1 or 2 doses`,`Licensed private schedule often 3 doses`],[`21 and over`,`2 doses 6 months apart`,`Catch-up to 26; 27-45 shared decision`],[`HIV or immunocompromised`,`At least 2, preferably 3 doses`,`Screen from 25 every 3-5 years`]]}]},"gynaecology-menopause-hormone-therapy":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Menopause is permanent cessation of menses from **loss of ovarian follicles**.`,`It is a clinical diagnosis after **12 months of amenorrhoea**; no blood test makes it.`,`Mean age in Indian women is **46-47 years**, earlier than the Western 51.`,`**POI** is menopause before 40; early menopause is 40-45.`,`Both POI and early menopause need hormone replacement **until about 51**.`,`Vasomotor symptoms affect about 75% and last a median of 7.4 years.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use **STRAW+10** staging: reproductive, menopausal transition, postmenopause.`,`Early transition (-2): cycle lengths differ by 7 days or more.`,`Late transition (-1): amenorrhoea of 60 days or more, FSH over 25 IU/L.`,`Stage 0 is the final period, confirmed after 12 months.`,`Menopause Rating Scale: 17 or more is severe.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Follicles fall from 1-2 million at birth to about 1000 at menopause.`,`2. Inhibin B falls first, so FSH rises while oestradiol swings widely.`,`3. Oestrogen loss makes **KNDy neurons** overactive in the hypothalamus.`,`4. **Neurokinin B** on NK3 receptors narrows the thermoneutral zone, causing flushes.`,`5. Vaginal epithelium thins and pH rises, giving **genitourinary syndrome (GSM)**.`,`6. Loss of oestrogen frees osteoclasts; bone falls 2-5% a year for 5-7 years.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask age, LMP, cycle change, flushes, night sweats, mood and sleep.`,`Indian women often lead with **body aches, burning feet and palpitations**.`,`Ask directly about vaginal dryness, dyspareunia and recurrent UTI.`,`**Any bleeding after 12 months** needs endometrial assessment before hormones.`,`Screen MHT risks: breast cancer, VTE, stroke, migraine with aura, liver disease.`,`Contraception is still needed: **12 months after last period if over 50, 24 if under 50**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record weight, BP and height; **height loss over 4 cm** suggests vertebral fracture.`,`Check thyroid and pulse to exclude thyrotoxicosis.`,`**Breast examination** before starting MHT.`,`Pelvis: pale, thin, dry mucosa with loss of rugae; look for prolapse.`,`Do cervical screening if due.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Over 45 with typical symptoms: no FSH**.`,`FSH only at 40-45, suspected POI, or when periods cannot be judged.`,`POI: FSH over 25 IU/L; ESHRE 2024 accepts a single value, NICE asks for two.`,`Baseline Hb, glucose, lipids, TSH and vitamin D where deficiency is likely.`,`**DXA** for risk factors; osteoporosis is a T score of -2.5 or below.`,`Postmenopausal bleeding: TVS; endometrium **over 4 mm** needs sampling.`,`POI work-up: karyotype, **FMR1 premutation**, autoimmune screen and DXA.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Lifestyle: 150 minutes of exercise a week, stop smoking, limit alcohol.`,`Offer **CBT** for flush bother, low mood and anxiety.`,`**MHT** is most effective when started **under 60 or within 10 years** of menopause.`,`**Uterus present** means add a progestogen; hysterectomy means oestrogen alone.`,`**Transdermal oestradiol** does not raise VTE risk; prefer it in obesity, migraine, hypertension.`,`**Micronised progesterone** is the preferred progestogen, taken at bedtime.`,`There is **no fixed 5-year limit**; review yearly at the lowest effective dose.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`GSM: **local vaginal oestrogen**, no progestogen needed, continue indefinitely.`,`Non-hormonal: venlafaxine, escitalopram, gabapentin, clonidine.`,`**Fezolinetant 45 mg** daily (NK3 antagonist); check LFT monthly for 3 months.`,`Elinzanetant (NK1/NK3) was FDA-approved in October 2025; not yet in India.`,`On tamoxifen, avoid **paroxetine**; use venlafaxine or escitalopram.`,`Osteoporosis: **alendronate 70 mg weekly**; zoledronic acid or denosumab as alternatives.`,`**POI**: higher-dose oestrogen plus progestogen, or a combined pill, until at least 51.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Absolute contraindications: undiagnosed bleeding, breast cancer, VTE, active arterial disease.`,`Bleeding beyond 6 months of continuous combined MHT needs TVS and biopsy.`,`Stop oestrogen at once for calf pain, chest pain or sudden severe headache.`,`Breast risk is mainly from the progestogen: 1 extra per 1000 per year after 5 years.`,`FDA (November 2025) removed CVD, breast cancer and dementia from the MHT boxed warning.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review **3 months** after starting or changing MHT, then yearly.`,`Taper MHT over 3-6 months rather than stopping abruptly.`,`Refer urgently for postmenopausal bleeding or a breast lump.`,`Refer POI and women with contraindications who still have severe symptoms.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in menopause`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Transdermal oestradiol`,`Patch 25-50 microgram/24 h twice weekly, or gel 0.75-1.5 mg daily`,`Review yearly`,`Skin irritation; no VTE excess`],[`Oestradiol valerate`,`1-2 mg oral daily (POI 2-4 mg)`,`Review yearly; POI until 51`,`VTE, gallstones, stroke over 60`],[`Micronised progesterone`,`100 mg nightly, or 200 mg nightly 12-14 days a month`,`With oestrogen if uterus present`,`Drowsiness; take at bedtime`],[`Tibolone`,`2.5 mg oral daily`,`Over 12 months postmenopause`,`Stroke over 60, breast cancer recurrence`],[`Vaginal estriol or oestradiol`,`Estriol 0.5 mg or E2 10 microgram vaginally`,`Daily 2 weeks, then twice weekly`,`Minimal systemic effect; no progestogen`],[`Fezolinetant`,`45 mg oral daily`,`Long term`,`Rare liver injury; LFT monthly for 3 months`],[`Venlafaxine`,`37.5-75 mg oral daily`,`Effect within 2 weeks; taper to stop`,`Nausea, BP rise; safe with tamoxifen`],[`Gabapentin`,`300 mg at night, up to 900 mg`,`Long term`,`Dizziness, drowsiness`],[`Alendronate`,`70 mg oral weekly, fasting, upright 30 min`,`Review after 5 years`,`Oesophagitis, rare ONJ`],[`Calcium + vitamin D3`,`Calcium 500-1000 mg + D3 800-1000 IU daily`,`Long term`,`Constipation`]]},{heading:`Choosing the MHT regimen`,columns:[`Situation`,`Regimen`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Uterus, within 12 months of last period`,`Sequential combined`,`Monthly bleed; needs contraception`],[`Uterus, over 12 months since last period`,`Continuous combined`,`Bleed-free; bleeding after 6 months needs tests`],[`After hysterectomy`,`Oestrogen alone`,`No progestogen`],[`GSM only`,`Local vaginal oestrogen`,`No progestogen; continue indefinitely`],[`POI (under 40)`,`Full replacement or combined pill`,`Continue to at least 51`]]},{heading:`STRAW+10 stages`,columns:[`Stage`,`Phase`,`Defining criteria`],rows:[[`-2`,`Early transition`,`Cycle lengths differ by 7 days or more`],[`-1`,`Late transition`,`Amenorrhoea 60 days or more; FSH over 25`],[`0`,`Final menstrual period`,`Confirmed after 12 months`],[`+1`,`Early postmenopause`,`First 6 years; flushes most likely`],[`+2`,`Late postmenopause`,`Genitourinary symptoms increase`]]}],remember:[`Over 45 with typical symptoms, **ordering FSH is the error**.`,`The **uterus decides** the regimen: uterus means add progestogen.`,`Transdermal oestradiol has no VTE excess.`,`Window: under 60 or within 10 years; no fixed 5-year limit.`,`POI is replacement until at least 51, not optional.`,`GSM never improves alone: local oestrogen, indefinitely.`,`On tamoxifen: never paroxetine.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`US FDA (Nov 2025): cardiovascular disease, breast cancer and probable dementia removed from the boxed warning on menopausal hormone therapy; endometrial cancer warning for unopposed oestrogen retained.`,`US FDA (Oct 2025): elinzanetant (dual NK1/NK3 antagonist) approved for moderate-severe vasomotor symptoms - added alongside fezolinetant as a non-hormonal option.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - menopause and MHT`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Permanent cessation of menses from **follicle loss**, **retrospective diagnosis after 12 months of amenorrhoea**; mean age **46-47 in India** (51 in West); **POI under 40 (about 1%)**, early menopause 40-45 - **replace until about 51**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**STRAW+10**: -3 late reproductive, **-2 early transition (7+ day cycle variability)**, **-1 late transition (60+ days amenorrhoea, FSH over 25)**, 0 FMP, +1 early postmenopause, +2 late; Menopause Rating Scale (0-44, 17+ severe).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Natural follicle depletion; POI - idiopathic, **Turner, fragile X premutation**, autoimmune, **oophorectomy, chemotherapy, radiotherapy**; **smoking advances menopause by 2 years**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Follicles fall -> **inhibin B falls -> FSH rises** (oestradiol swings) -> oestrogen loss -> **KNDy neuron hyperactivity -> neurokinin B on NK3 receptors -> narrowed thermoneutral zone -> flushes**; RANKL rise -> bone loss **2-5%/year**; GSM; LDL rise.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`LMP and cycle change, **flushes, night sweats, sleep, mood**, aches; **GSM (dryness, dyspareunia, urgency, UTIs)**; **PMB**; MHT suitability - **breast/endometrial cancer, VTE, stroke/IHD, migraine with aura, liver**; tamoxifen; fracture risk; contraception need.`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, waist, **BP**, height (loss over 4 cm), thyroid; **breast examination**; pelvic - pale dry mucosa (GSM), prolapse; cervical screening if due; kyphosis, gait.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Over 45 with typical symptoms: no FSH**; FSH only 40-45 or **POI under 40 (FSH over 25 IU/L - one value enough, ESHRE 2024; repeat/AMH if unclear)**; Hb, glucose, lipids, TSH, vitamin D; **DXA (T -2.5 or below)** if risk/FRAX; **TVS for PMB - over 4 mm needs sampling**.`],[`Treatment - general`,`Lifestyle, **150 min/week exercise incl. resistance**, stop smoking, **CBT**, cool clothing; **calcium 1000-1200 mg/day with vitamin D3 800-1000 IU**; vaginal moisturisers/lubricants for mild GSM; no proof for phytoestrogens/black cohosh.`],[`Treatment - first line (MHT)`,`**Start under 60 or within 10 years of menopause**, no fixed 5-year limit; **transdermal oestradiol patch 25-50 microgram twice weekly or gel 0.75-1.5 mg daily** (no VTE excess) or oral E2 1-2 mg; **uterus: + micronised progesterone 100 mg nightly continuous or 200 mg 12-14 days/month**, or LNG-IUS 52 mg.`],[`Regimen choice`,`**Hysterectomy - oestrogen alone**; uterus + within 12 months of LMP - **sequential**; over 12 months - **continuous combined**; GSM alone - **estriol 0.5 mg / E2 10 microgram vaginal daily 2 weeks then twice weekly, no progestogen**; tibolone 2.5 mg over 12 months postmenopause.`],[`Non-hormonal options`,`**Fezolinetant 45 mg daily (NK3)**, **elinzanetant 120 mg at bedtime (NK1/NK3, FDA Oct 2025)** - LFT monitoring; **venlafaxine 37.5-75 mg or escitalopram 10-20 mg** (safe with tamoxifen); **avoid paroxetine/fluoxetine with tamoxifen**; gabapentin 300-900 mg at night; clonidine.`],[`Contraindications`,`**Undiagnosed bleeding, breast or oestrogen-dependent cancer, VTE/thrombophilia, active arterial disease, active liver disease, untreated hyperplasia**, pregnancy; relative cautions (migraine, gallstones, BP, obesity) -> **transdermal route, not refusal**.`],[`Special groups`,`**POI: E2 2-4 mg oral or 75-100 microgram patch + progestogen, or COC, until 51**; breast cancer survivors - non-hormonal, vaginal oestrogen with oncology agreement; **contraception 12 months after LMP if over 50, 24 if under 50**.`],[`Bone`,`**Alendronate 70 mg weekly** (on waking, full glass water, upright, fast 30 min; holiday review at 5 years); **zoledronic acid 5 mg IV yearly**; denosumab 60 mg 6-monthly - never stop without bisphosphonate.`],[`Complications / risks`,`Breast cancer mainly with **progestogen - about 1 extra/1000/year beyond 5 years**; **oral route doubles VTE**, stroke over 60; unopposed oestrogen -> endometrial cancer; bleeding beyond 6 months of continuous combined needs work-up.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**PMB (2-week pathway)**, breast lump, **calf swelling/chest pain/sudden headache on MHT - stop**, POI, severe symptoms with contraindications, mimics (thyrotoxicosis, TB, phaeochromocytoma).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **3 months after start**, then **yearly** (BP, weight, bleeding, breast, screening); taper over 3-6 months to stop; DXA every 2-3 years on treatment.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**FDA Nov 2025 removed CVD, breast cancer and dementia from the MHT boxed warning** (endometrial warning stays); **elinzanetant approved Oct 2025**; NICE NG23 (2024) remains the core guideline.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**No FSH over 45**; **uterus = progestogen**; **transdermal = no VTE excess**; window - under 60 or within 10 years; **GSM needs local oestrogen indefinitely, no progestogen**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Menopausal woman in the OPD - MHT decision`,steps:[{label:`Diagnose clinically over 45`,detail:`No FSH; FSH only 40-45 or under 40 (POI: FSH over 25 IU/L, ESHRE 2024)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Bleeding after 12 months?`,detail:`PMB -> TVS (over 4 mm) and biopsy before any hormone`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Absolute contraindication?`,detail:`Breast cancer, VTE, active arterial or liver disease, undiagnosed bleeding -> non-hormonal`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Under 60 or within 10 years?`,detail:`Benefits outweigh risks for symptomatic women; over 60 start cautiously, transdermal`},{label:`Choose regimen by uterus and LMP`,detail:`No uterus: E2 alone; uterus: sequential if under 12 months, continuous combined if over`},{label:`Review 3 months, then yearly`,detail:`Lowest effective dose, no fixed stop date; taper to stop`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Vasomotor symptom options compared`,columns:[`Option`,`Dose`,`Key caution`],rows:[[`Transdermal oestradiol (+ progestogen if uterus)`,`Patch 25-50 microgram twice weekly or gel 0.75-1.5 mg daily`,`Most effective; contraindications as listed`],[`Fezolinetant (NK3)`,`45 mg once daily`,`LFT baseline and monthly for 3 months`],[`Elinzanetant (NK1/NK3)`,`120 mg once daily at bedtime (FDA 2025)`,`Somnolence, headache; liver enzymes; not marketed in India`],[`Venlafaxine / escitalopram`,`37.5-75 mg / 10-20 mg daily`,`Safe with tamoxifen; avoid paroxetine with tamoxifen`],[`Gabapentin`,`300 mg at night, up to 900 mg`,`Dizziness, drowsiness; good for night sweats`]]}]},"gynaecology-uterine-prolapse":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Prolapse is descent of a vaginal wall, the uterus or the vault **towards or through the introitus**.`,`Describe it **by compartment**: anterior, apical or posterior.`,`Anterior: urethrocele and cystocele; apical: uterine or vault descent, enterocele.`,`Posterior: rectocele and deficient perineum.`,`Lifetime risk of surgery for prolapse or incontinence is **12-20%**.`,`In India it presents a decade earlier: high parity, unattended births, early heavy work.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**POP-Q** measures points in cm from the hymen on maximum Valsalva.`,`Stages 0-IV are set by the **leading edge**.`,`Clinic use: **Baden-Walker** halfway system, grades 1-4.`,`Indian wards: **Shaw's degrees**; third degree is procidentia.`,`**Cervical elongation** (C minus D over 4 cm) is not true descent.`,`**Round and broad ligaments are not supports**; naming them loses the mark.`,`The **cardinal (Mackenrodt) ligament** is the strongest single mechanical support.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The **levator ani** keeps the levator plate horizontal and the hiatus closed.`,`2. Childbirth tears or denervates the levator (S3-S4, pudendal nerve).`,`3. The plate sags and the genital hiatus widens; **muscle fails before ligament**.`,`4. Cardinal and uterosacral ligaments then stretch and give way.`,`5. Raised abdominal pressure (cough, constipation, load carrying) drives descent further.`,`6. Oestrogen loss after menopause weakens fascia and thins the epithelium.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Cardinal symptom: **'something coming down'**, worse by evening, gone on lying down.`,`Dragging discomfort and sacral backache relieved by lying flat.`,`Urinary: frequency, stress leak, poor stream, **pushing the lump back to void**.`,`Bowel: incomplete emptying and need to **digitate** suggest rectocele.`,`Ask whether she wishes to keep **coital function** and to have more children.`,`Rapid onset with weight loss or ascites suggests a tumour pushing from above.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examine the abdomen first for a mass or ascites.`,`Use a **Sims speculum** in left lateral to display each compartment.`,`Grade each compartment and note genital hiatus, perineal body and vaginal length.`,`**Reduce the prolapse and do a cough test** to unmask occult stress incontinence.`,`An ulcer with **rolled edges or induration** needs biopsy.`,`Rate pelvic floor strength on the modified Oxford scale 0-5.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Urine dipstick and culture, since residual urine invites infection.`,`**Post-void residual**; over about 100 mL means incomplete emptying.`,`Hb, glucose and **creatinine**; obstructive uropathy is easily missed.`,`**Renal ultrasound** is mandatory in procidentia or a large residual.`,`Urodynamics are **not routine**; only for complex symptoms or planned continence surgery.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Reassure: it is common, benign and treatable.`,`**Supervised pelvic floor training for at least 16 weeks** for stage I-II.`,`Teach 8-12 maximal holds of 6-10 seconds, three times a day.`,`Reduce load: weight loss, stop smoking, treat cough and constipation.`,`**Vaginal oestrogen** treats atrophy and ulcers, not the prolapse.`,`**Ring pessary** for women unfit for or declining surgery, or wanting more children.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Hysterectomy alone is not a prolapse operation**; always re-suspend the vault.`,`Vaginal hysterectomy with repair and **McCall culdoplasty** once the family is complete.`,`**Manchester repair** for cervical elongation when the uterus is to be kept.`,`Sacrocolpopexy is the most durable option for vault or recurrent prolapse.`,`**Colpocleisis** for the frail elderly woman not wanting coital function.`,`Transvaginal mesh is restricted; abdominal mesh and mid-urethral slings are still allowed.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Procidentia with retention: **catheterise, reduce**, check creatinine and refer urgently.`,`Hydronephrosis occurs in up to a third of long-standing procidentia.`,`Decubitus ulcer heals in 2-3 weeks with reduction and oestrogen; operate on healed tissue.`,`A neglected pessary can cause impaction and **fistula**.`,`Prolapse in a nulliparous woman: look for a mass, ascites or connective tissue disease.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Pessary review at 1-2 weeks, then **every 3-6 months**; never fit without booked follow-up.`,`No heavy lifting for 6-12 weeks after surgery.`,`Refer stage III-IV, failed conservative care, young women and recurrence.`,`Prevent it: no pushing before full dilatation, repair tears, birth spacing.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and devices in prolapse care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Estriol vaginal cream`,`0.5 mg vaginally at bedtime`,`Daily 2 weeks, then twice weekly`,`Local irritation; no progestogen needed`],[`Oestradiol vaginal tablet`,`10 microgram vaginally at bedtime`,`Daily 2 weeks, then twice weekly`,`Spotting, local irritation`],[`Ispaghula husk`,`3.5 g oral once or twice daily in water`,`Long term`,`Bloating; obstruction if taken dry`],[`Lactulose or PEG`,`Lactulose 15 mL twice daily or PEG 17 g daily`,`Titrate to soft stool`,`Bloating, cramps`],[`Mirabegron`,`25-50 mg oral daily`,`Long term`,`Raises BP; check BP`],[`Nitrofurantoin MR`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`3 days`,`Avoid if eGFR under 45`],[`Cefazolin`,`2 g IV within 60 min before incision`,`Single dose`,`Allergy`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC daily from 6-12 h after surgery`,`Until mobile`,`Bleeding, bruising`],[`Ring pessary`,`Vaginal device, usually 60-75 mm`,`Clean or change every 3-6 months`,`Discharge, erosion, fistula if neglected`]]},{heading:`POP-Q stages and management`,columns:[`Stage`,`Leading edge`,`Older grade`,`Usual management`],rows:[[`0`,`No descent`,`Baden-Walker 0`,`Reassure`],[`I`,`More than 1 cm above hymen`,`First degree`,`Supervised PFMT 16 weeks`],[`II`,`Within 1 cm of hymen`,`Second degree`,`PFMT, oestrogen, pessary`],[`III`,`More than 1 cm below hymen`,`Baden-Walker 3`,`Pessary or surgery`],[`IV`,`Complete eversion`,`Procidentia`,`Reduce, renal scan, surgery`]]},{heading:`DeLancey levels of support`,columns:[`Level`,`Support`,`Failure gives`],rows:[[`I - apex`,`Cardinal-uterosacral complex`,`Uterine or vault descent, enterocele`],[`II - mid-vagina`,`Pubocervical and rectovaginal fascia to white line`,`Cystocele, rectocele`],[`III - distal`,`Perineal body, perineal membrane`,`Urethrocele, deficient perineum`]]},{heading:`Choosing the operation`,columns:[`Operation`,`Best indication`,`Main caveat`],rows:[[`Vaginal hysterectomy + McCall`,`Uterovaginal prolapse, family complete`,`Vault must be re-suspended`],[`Manchester repair`,`Cervical elongation, keep uterus`,`Stenosis, subfertility`],[`Sacrospinous fixation`,`Vault prolapse by vaginal route`,`Buttock pain; pudendal injury`],[`Anterior colporrhaphy`,`Cystocele`,`Highest recurrence`],[`Sacrocolpopexy`,`Vault or recurrent prolapse`,`Most durable, most morbid`],[`Colpocleisis`,`Frail elderly, no coitus`,`Irreversible`]]}],remember:[`**Round ligament is not a support**; it only keeps anteversion.`,`Ligaments are the moorings, the levator plate is the berth.`,`DeLancey: **I apex, II mid-vagina, III perineum**.`,`Hysterectomy alone is not a prolapse operation.`,`Reduce and cough: occult stress incontinence in a fifth to a third.`,`C minus D over 4 cm: Manchester repair, not hysterectomy.`,`Water under the bridge: ureter under the uterine artery.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - pelvic organ prolapse`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Descent of the **anterior or posterior vaginal wall, uterus/cervix or vault towards or through the introitus**, with symptoms; **procidentia** = whole uterus outside; lifetime surgery risk 12-20%, presents a decade earlier in India.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**POP-Q (ICS/IUGA)**: points Aa, Ba, C, D, Ap, Bp, gh, pb, tvl; **stage 0-IV by leading edge** (I over 1 cm above hymen, II within 1 cm, III over 1 cm below, IV complete eversion); clinic - **Baden-Walker**, Shaw degrees; by **compartment** (anterior, apical, posterior).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Vaginal birth (levator avulsion, pudendal injury)**, prolonged second stage, **pushing before full dilatation, fundal pressure**, high parity, short intervals; **chronic cough, constipation, obesity, heavy load carrying**; menopause; connective tissue disorders in nullipara.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Levator ani damage -> **levator plate sags, hiatus widens (gh 4 cm or more)** -> load shifts to ligaments -> **DeLancey Level I failure (uterine/vault, enterocele), Level II (cystocele/rectocele), Level III (perineum)** -> descent.`],[`Supports (viva)`,`**Levator ani = main support**; **cardinal ligament strongest single ligament**; uterosacral pulls cervix back; **round and broad ligaments are not supports**; ureter 1.5-2 cm lateral to cervix under uterine artery.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**'Something coming down'**, worse by evening, **disappears lying down**; dragging, sacral backache; urinary - SUI, **pushing lump back to void**, retention; bowel - **digitation** (rectocele); sexual function wish; bleeding/discharge (ulcer); obstetric history; cough, load carrying; fertility wish.`],[`Key examination`,`Abdomen for mass/ascites first; **dorsal at rest and straining, then Sims speculum** each compartment; **POP-Q or Baden-Walker**; **reduce and cough test (occult SUI)**; ulcer edge (biopsy if rolled/indurated); cervical elongation; rectovaginal for enterocele; **Oxford 0-5** floor strength.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Urine culture**, **post-void residual (over 100 mL abnormal)**, Hb, glucose, **creatinine**, **renal USG (mandatory in procidentia)**; cervical screening; **TVS and endometrial sampling for PMB before surgery/colpocleisis**; urodynamics not routine.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Supervised PFMT for stage I-II - at least 16 weeks** (8-12 maximal holds of 6-10 s, three times daily) + 'the knack'; weight loss, stop smoking, treat cough and constipation; **vaginal oestrogen (estriol 0.5 mg or E2 10 microgram, nightly 2 weeks then twice weekly)** for atrophy - does not lift prolapse.`],[`Pessary`,`**Ring pessary 60-75 mm first choice** (stage I-III, intact perineum); space-filling (Gellhorn, cube) for severe; check she can **walk, cough, squat, void**; review 1-2 weeks then **every 3-6 months**; for unfit, pregnant, awaiting surgery, ulcer healing.`],[`Surgery`,`**Vaginal hysterectomy + repair + McCall culdoplasty** (vault must be suspended); **Manchester repair for cervical elongation**; anterior colporrhaphy (highest recurrence), posterior colpoperineorrhaphy; **sacrospinous fixation/hysteropexy**; **sacrocolpopexy most durable** (vault/recurrent); Purandare/Shirodkar slings; **colpocleisis** for frail, no coitus; **no transvaginal mesh (NICE NG123)**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Young woman wanting children**: PFMT, pessary, uterus-preserving sling/hysteropexy; **nullipara**: exclude connective tissue disease and abdominal mass; **frail elderly**: pessary or colpocleisis under regional anaesthesia.`],[`Acute / emergency`,`**Procidentia with retention**: catheterise, reduce, hold with pessary or pack, creatinine, renal USG, urgent referral; irreducible/oedematous - elevate, cold and **glycerine-magnesium sulphate packs**; impacted pessary - remove, check for fistula.`],[`Complications`,`**Decubitus ulcer**, **hydroureter/hydronephrosis (up to a third of procidentia)**, retention, recurrent UTI, rarely squamous carcinoma; post-surgery recurrence **10-30% in 5 years**, mesh exposure.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Same day: **retention, rising creatinine, hydronephrosis, irreducible/gangrenous prolapse**; urgent: non-healing or suspicious ulcer, PMB; routine: stage III-IV, failed conservative care, nullipara, recurrence.`],[`Prevention`,`No pushing before full dilatation, **no fundal pressure**, correct instrument use, **anatomical perineal repair**, postnatal PFMT, avoid heavy work 3 months, **birth spacing**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Hysterectomy alone is not a prolapse operation**; **cardinal ligament is the strongest support, round ligament is not a support**; Manchester for elongation; **renal scan in every procidentia**; never fit a pessary without follow-up.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Prolapse assessment in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Abdomen first`,detail:`Exclude mass or ascites pushing from above`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Examine at rest and on straining`,detail:`Dorsal then Sims speculum; each compartment; standing if history does not match`},{label:`Grade (POP-Q or Baden-Walker)`,detail:`Leading edge vs hymen; note gh, pb, tvl, cervical elongation`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Reduce and cough test`,detail:`Unmask occult stress incontinence before surgery`},{label:`Residual, urine culture, creatinine`,detail:`PVR over 100 mL abnormal; renal USG in procidentia or large residual`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Match treatment to stage and wishes`,detail:`Age, fertility, coital function, compartments, fitness`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Prolapse treatment ladder`,steps:[{label:`Lifestyle and load reduction`,detail:`Weight, smoking, cough, constipation, lifting`,tone:`good`},{label:`Supervised PFMT 16 weeks`,detail:`Stage I-II; 8-12 holds of 6-10 s three times daily; the knack`},{label:`Vaginal oestrogen + ring pessary`,detail:`Estriol 0.5 mg nightly 2 weeks then twice weekly; ring 60-75 mm; review every 3-6 months`},{label:`Reconstructive surgery`,detail:`VH + repair + McCall; Manchester; sacrospinous; sacrocolpopexy for vault - no transvaginal mesh`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Colpocleisis`,detail:`Frail elderly, no coitus wished; over 90% success; exclude cervical and endometrial disease first`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"gynaecology-amenorrhoea-menstrual-irregularity":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Primary amenorrhoea**: no menses by **15 years** with normal secondary sexual characters.`,`Also primary: no menses by **13** with no breasts, or 3 years after thelarche.`,`**Secondary amenorrhoea**: no menses for **3 months** after regular or 6 months after irregular cycles.`,`**Pregnancy** is the commonest cause of secondary amenorrhoea.`,`Cycles over 38 days are infrequent and fall under **AUB-O** of PALM-COEIN.`,`Irregularity in the first year after menarche is normal.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use the **compartment approach**: I outflow tract, II ovary, III pituitary, IV hypothalamus.`,`Add other endocrine causes: PCOS, thyroid, non-classic CAH, Cushing.`,`Primary: **Turner (40-50%)**, MRKH (about 15%), constitutional delay.`,`Secondary: PCOS, hypothalamic, hyperprolactinaemia, POI, thyroid, Asherman, genital TB.`,`In India, think of **genital TB and curettage** after previously normal periods.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A period needs pulsatile GnRH, pituitary FSH and LH, a working ovary and a patent outflow.`,`2. Menstruation is **progesterone withdrawal** from an oestrogen-primed endometrium.`,`3. Stress, low energy and weight loss slow GnRH pulses, giving **hypothalamic amenorrhoea**.`,`4. **Prolactin suppresses GnRH**; dopamine-blocking drugs raise prolactin.`,`5. Ovarian failure removes feedback, so **FSH rises above 25 IU/L**.`,`6. Curettage or TB destroys the basal endometrium, causing **Asherman syndrome**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask thelarche, menarche, LMP and, privately, sexual activity and contraception.`,`Ask weight change, diet, exercise, stress and eating-disorder symptoms.`,`Galactorrhoea, headache or visual disturbance suggest a pituitary tumour.`,`Hirsutism and acne suggest PCOS; **rapid virilisation** suggests a tumour.`,`Cyclical pain with retention in a girl without periods suggests outflow obstruction.`,`Ask about D and C, TB, **PPH with failed lactation** (Sheehan) and drugs.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record height, BMI and BP; short stature suggests Turner.`,`**Tanner staging**: absent breasts mean no oestrogen ever.`,`**Normal breasts with no pubic hair** suggest androgen insensitivity.`,`Look for Turner stigmata: webbed neck, shield chest, cubitus valgus.`,`Check visual fields, smell (Kallmann) and galactorrhoea.`,`Inspect for a bulging bluish membrane (imperforate hymen) or short blind vagina.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Step 1: hCG** in every case, even if she denies sexual activity.`,`**Step 2: TSH, prolactin, FSH, LH, oestradiol** and pelvic ultrasound.`,`Prolactin over 25 ng/mL: repeat, exclude drugs and hypothyroidism, then MRI pituitary.`,`**High FSH with low oestradiol**: karyotype, FMR1 premutation, autoimmune screen.`,`Low or normal FSH with low oestradiol: hypothalamic or pituitary cause.`,`Renal ultrasound in MRKH; endometrial CBNAAT if genital TB is suspected.`,`DXA after 6 months or more of low-oestrogen amenorrhoea.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat the cause**: stop the drug, give levothyroxine, open an imperforate hymen.`,`PCOS: **5-10% weight loss** and at least 150 minutes of exercise a week.`,`Hypothalamic amenorrhoea: restore energy balance and offer CBT.`,`Chronic anovulation: withdrawal bleed **at least every 3 months** with MPA 10 mg for 12-14 days.`,`PCOS cycle control: a **COC**; hirsutism improves only after 6 months.`,`Prolactinoma: **cabergoline 0.25 mg twice weekly**, with food at bedtime.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**POI and Turner**: oestradiol plus cyclic progestogen **until about 51**.`,`Turner puberty induction with low-dose transdermal oestradiol from 11-12 years.`,`Hypothalamic amenorrhoea with low bone density: transdermal oestradiol, not the pill.`,`PCOS infertility: **letrozole 2.5 mg days 3-7** is first line.`,`Genital TB: 2HRZE/4HRE under NTEP for 6 months.`,`MRKH: **vaginal dilators** first; surrogacy permitted under the 2021 Act.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Adolescents: irregular cycles in the first 2 years are usually anovulatory; reassure.`,`Adolescent PCOS needs both anovulation and hyperandrogenism.`,`**Swyer syndrome** (46,XY, streak gonads) needs gonadectomy for tumour risk.`,`Disclose MRKH, Turner and AIS diagnoses carefully with counselling.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Headache with field loss: pituitary tumour.`,`BMI under 15, pulse under 40 or syncope: severe anorexia; admit.`,`Failed lactation and amenorrhoea after PPH: **Sheehan**, with adrenal crisis risk.`,`Ambiguous genitalia or an inguinal gonad: disorder of sex development.`,`Refer **all primary amenorrhoea** after the initial work-up.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in amenorrhoea and irregular cycles`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Medroxyprogesterone acetate`,`10 mg oral daily`,`12-14 days every 1-3 months`,`Bloating; bleed 2-7 days after stopping`],[`Combined oral contraceptive`,`EE 20-30 microgram, 1 tablet daily`,`Long term`,`BP rise, rare VTE; check MEC`],[`Metformin`,`500 mg oral, up to 1500-2000 mg/day`,`Long term`,`GI upset, B12 deficiency`],[`Cabergoline`,`0.25-1 mg oral twice weekly`,`At least 2 years`,`Nausea, dizziness`],[`Bromocriptine`,`1.25 mg at night, up to 2.5 mg BD-TDS`,`Preferred if pregnancy planned`,`Nausea, postural hypotension`],[`Letrozole`,`2.5-7.5 mg oral days 3-7`,`Up to 6 cycles`,`Hot flushes; needs follicle tracking`],[`Oestradiol (POI, Turner)`,`2 mg oral or 100 microgram patch daily`,`Until about 51, with progestogen`,`VTE with oral; never unopposed with uterus`],[`Spironolactone`,`50-100 mg oral daily`,`After 6 months of COC`,`Hyperkalaemia; teratogenic`],[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 microgram/kg/day oral`,`Recheck TSH at 6 weeks`,`Empty stomach before breakfast`]]},{heading:`Compartment classification`,columns:[`Compartment`,`Examples`,`Key finding`],rows:[[`I - Outflow and uterus`,`Imperforate hymen, MRKH, AIS, Asherman, TB`,`Normal FSH and oestradiol`],[`II - Ovary`,`Turner, Swyer, POI`,`FSH over 25 IU/L, low oestradiol`],[`III - Pituitary`,`Prolactinoma, Sheehan`,`High prolactin or low FSH and LH`],[`IV - Hypothalamus`,`Stress, weight loss, anorexia, Kallmann`,`Low or normal FSH, low oestradiol`],[`Other endocrine`,`PCOS, thyroid, CAH, Cushing`,`High LH, androgens, abnormal TSH`]]},{heading:`Primary amenorrhoea: telling causes apart`,columns:[`Condition`,`Karyotype`,`Breasts and hair`,`Uterus`,`FSH`],rows:[[`Turner`,`45,X`,`Absent breasts`,`Present, small`,`High`],[`MRKH`,`46,XX`,`Normal`,`Absent`,`Normal`],[`Complete AIS`,`46,XY`,`Breasts normal, hair scanty`,`Absent`,`Normal or high`],[`Swyer`,`46,XY`,`Absent breasts`,`Present`,`High`],[`Imperforate hymen`,`46,XX`,`Normal`,`Present, haematometra`,`Normal`],[`Constitutional delay or Kallmann`,`46,XX`,`Absent or early`,`Present`,`Low`]]}],remember:[`Every amenorrhoea is **pregnancy until proved otherwise**.`,`After hCG: TSH, prolactin, FSH/LH, oestradiol, ultrasound.`,`Turner is the commonest cause of primary amenorrhoea.`,`Normal breasts, no pubic hair, blind vagina: **androgen insensitivity**.`,`Y chromosome with streak gonads: gonadectomy.`,`Chronic anovulation: a bleed at least every 3 months.`,`POI: HRT until about 51.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - amenorrhoea and menstrual irregularity`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Primary**: no menarche by **15 with normal secondary sex characters**, **13 with none**, or 3 years after thelarche; **secondary**: no menses **3 months (regular) or 6 months (irregular)**; **pregnancy is the commonest cause**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Compartments**: **I outflow tract/uterus**, **II ovary**, **III anterior pituitary**, **IV hypothalamus/CNS**, plus other endocrine (PCOS, thyroid, NC-CAH, Cushing); FIGO 2018 normal cycle **24-38 days**, variation 7-9 days or less.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Primary: **gonadal dysgenesis 40-50% (Turner)**, **MRKH about 15%**, constitutional delay, Kallmann, AIS, imperforate hymen; secondary: **PCOS, FHA, hyperprolactinaemia, POI, thyroid, Asherman, genital TB, Sheehan**, drugs (antipsychotics, DMPA).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Period needs **pulsatile GnRH -> FSH/LH -> ovarian E2 then progesterone -> responsive endometrium -> patent outflow**; stress/low energy slow GnRH (FHA); **prolactin suppresses kisspeptin/GnRH**; ovarian failure -> **FSH over 25 IU/L**; low oestrogen -> bone loss.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`**Sexual activity and LMP privately**; thelarche/menarche; **weight, diet, exercise, stress, eating disorder**; **galactorrhoea, headache, visual loss**; hirsutism, virilisation; **flushes**; **cyclical pain with retention** (obstruction); D and C, TB, **PPH with failed lactation (Sheehan)**; drugs, chemo/radiotherapy; anosmia.`],[`Key examination`,`Height, BMI (under 18.5 FHA), BP; **Tanner staging - breasts = oestrogen, pubic hair = androgen**; **Turner stigmata**; mFG, acanthosis, virilisation; visual fields, smell, galactorrhoea; **external genitalia - bulging bluish hymen, blind vagina, inguinal gonads**; eating-disorder signs.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**Step 1 hCG always**; **step 2 TSH, prolactin, FSH, LH, E2**; prolactin over 25 ng/mL - repeat, exclude macroprolactin/drugs/hypothyroid, MRI; **FSH over 25 + low E2 -> karyotype, FMR1, autoantibodies**; low FSH/LH -> MRI if no functional cause; pelvic USG (renal USG in MRKH); testosterone, DHEAS, 17-OHP; DXA after 6 months.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**Progestogen challenge (MPA 10 mg 7-10 days)**: bleed = oestrogen present + patent tract; no bleed after **oestrogen + progestogen = outflow/endometrial cause**; primary amenorrhoea - **uterus present? breasts present? FSH high or low? karyotype**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Treat the cause**: stop drug, levothyroxine 1.6 microgram/kg, **hymenotomy**, **weight restoration and CBT for FHA**; PCOS - lifestyle, **COC** or **MPA 10 mg 12-14 days every 1-3 months** to protect endometrium, metformin; **cabergoline 0.25 mg twice weekly -> 0.5-1 mg** (bromocriptine if pregnancy planned).`],[`Escalation / second line`,`**POI/Turner HRT: E2 2 mg oral or 100 microgram patch + cyclic progestogen until 51**; **puberty induction low-dose transdermal E2 from 11-12 years**; FHA over 6 months with low BMD - transdermal E2 (not COC); **letrozole** for PCOS infertility; genital TB **2HRZE/4HRE**; Asherman - hysteroscopic adhesiolysis.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**MRKH: vaginal dilators (Frank) first**, vaginoplasty if fail, surrogacy; **gonadectomy in Swyer** (gonadoblastoma) and after puberty in complete AIS; hysteroscopy/HSG for Asherman.`],[`Special groups`,`**Adolescents**: irregular first 2 years normal - calendar, WIFS iron; **athletes/anorexia**: female athlete triad, DXA; **lactation** physiological; **Turner**: lifelong cardiac (aortic dilatation), renal, hearing, thyroid follow-up.`],[`Complications`,`**Osteoporosis** (hypo-oestrogenic), endometrial hyperplasia (anovulation), infertility, **gonadoblastoma (Y material)**, psychological and identity distress, pituitary apoplexy/visual loss.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Refer all primary amenorrhoea after initial work-up**; pituitary tumour, POI, virilisation, DSD, Asherman, TB; **urgent: BMI under 15, pulse under 40, syncope, electrolyte disturbance, visual field loss, haematocolpos with retention**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`PCOS 6-12 monthly, OGTT 1-3 yearly, **bleed every 3 months**; prolactin 1 month after dose change; POI/Turner yearly, **DXA every 3-5 years**; FHA - weight, cycles, DXA at 12 months.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**RKSK adolescent-friendly health clinics, Menstrual Hygiene Scheme, WIFS**; eating-disorder awareness; avoid unnecessary curettage (Asherman); TB control.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Pregnancy test first, always**; **breasts without pubic hair = AIS**; **no uterus + normal breasts = MRKH**; FSH over 25 = ovarian failure -> karyotype; **Swyer has a uterus - remove the gonads**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Secondary amenorrhoea - stepwise work-up`,steps:[{label:`hCG`,detail:`Pregnancy is the commonest cause - test even if she denies sex`,tone:`warn`},{label:`TSH, prolactin, FSH, LH, E2`,detail:`Plus testosterone and 17-OHP if hyperandrogenic`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Prolactin high`,detail:`Repeat unstressed, exclude drugs, hypothyroid, macroprolactin -> MRI pituitary -> cabergoline`},{label:`FSH over 25 IU/L, low E2`,detail:`POI: karyotype, FMR1, autoantibodies, DXA; HRT until 51`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Low or normal FSH/LH, low E2`,detail:`FHA (weight, stress, exercise) or pituitary; MRI if headache or no functional cause`},{label:`Normal hormones`,detail:`PCOS (androgen, cycles) or outflow cause (Asherman, TB) - hysteroscopy, endometrial CBNAAT`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Primary amenorrhoea look-alikes`,columns:[`Condition`,`Karyotype`,`Breasts / pubic hair`,`Uterus`,`FSH`],rows:[[`Turner`,`45,X or mosaic`,`Absent / sparse`,`Present, small`,`High`],[`MRKH`,`46,XX`,`Normal / normal`,`Absent`,`Normal`],[`Complete AIS`,`46,XY`,`Normal / absent or scanty`,`Absent`,`Normal or high`],[`Swyer`,`46,XY`,`Absent / sparse`,`Present`,`High`],[`Imperforate hymen`,`46,XX`,`Normal / normal`,`Present, haematometra`,`Normal`],[`Constitutional delay or Kallmann`,`46,XX`,`Absent or early`,`Present`,`Low`]]}]},"gynaecology-ipv-sexual-assault":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**IPV** is physical, sexual, emotional or economic abuse or controlling behaviour by a partner.`,`NFHS-5: **29% of ever-married women** aged 18-49 reported spousal physical or sexual violence.`,`Only **14%** of them ever sought help.`,`**Rape** is a legal term decided by a court, not by the doctor.`,`Risk rises with husband's **alcohol use**, dowry demands, pregnancy and separation.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Physical**: slapping, beating, burning, strangling, acid attack.`,`**Sexual**: rape, forced sex by a partner, harassment, child sexual abuse.`,`**Emotional**: insults, threats, isolation, control.`,`**Economic**: denying money, taking earnings, dowry demands, eviction.`,`Structural: child marriage, honour crimes, trafficking, dowry deaths.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. IPV is a pattern of **power and control**, not isolated quarrels.`,`2. The **cycle of violence**: tension, acute assault, then reconciliation.`,`3. Economic dependence, children, stigma and fear explain why women stay.`,`4. Chronic threat keeps the HPA axis active, causing pain, insomnia and hypertension.`,`5. IPV roughly **doubles the risk of depression** and causes PTSD.`,`6. **Freezing** during assault is involuntary, so no struggle does not mean consent.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Suspect it with inconsistent injuries, delay, repeat visits or a partner who answers for her.`,`**Ask only when she is alone**, in private, after explaining confidentiality.`,`Ask directly: 'Has anyone at home ever hit or hurt you?'`,`Screening tools: **HITS** (Hurt, Insult, Threaten, Scream) or **HARK**.`,`Danger signs: threats to kill, weapons, **strangulation**, violence in pregnancy, separation.`,`Ask what she wants and **respect her decisions**; an adult woman decides on reporting.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Treat life-threatening injuries first**; evidence comes second.`,`Take separate consent for examination, evidence and police intimation; **12 or older can consent**.`,`Any registered doctor must examine; a female doctor is preferred but care is never delayed.`,`Record each injury on a **body map** with size, shape and site.`,`**The two-finger test is banned**; the Supreme Court called it misconduct (2022).`,`**Absence of injury does not rule out assault**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Urine pregnancy test before emergency contraception.`,`Baseline **HIV, HBsAg, anti-HCV and syphilis**, repeated at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months.`,`Creatinine before tenofovir PEP, without delaying the first dose.`,`Forensic evidence best within **96 hours**, with chain of custody.`,`Image the neck after strangulation with neurological symptoms.`,`Never order tests to 'prove' virginity.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**LIVES**: Listen, Inquire, Validate, Enhance safety, Support.`,`Do not blame her, push her to leave or suggest **couple counselling**.`,`Make a **safety plan**: safe place, emergency bag, code word, helpline numbers.`,`Document in her own words, register an MLC and give her a free copy.`,`Link to the **One Stop Centre (Sakhi)**, **181**, 112 and the Protection Officer.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Within 72 hours give **three time-critical drugs**: EC, STI prophylaxis and HIV PEP.`,`**Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg** once; repeat if vomited within 2 hours.`,`STI cover: azithromycin 1 g plus cefixime 400 mg plus metronidazole 2 g.`,`**PEP: TLD one tablet daily for 28 days**, started within 72 hours.`,`Hepatitis B vaccine if not immune; Td if wounds.`,`Pregnancy from rape: MTP allowed **up to 24 weeks**; preserve fetal tissue for DNA.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Children under 18: **POCSO reporting is mandatory** (section 19).`,`Burns in a young married woman suggest **dowry violence**; arrange a dying declaration if needed.`,`Violence in pregnancy causes abruption, preterm labour and fetal injury.`,`Children who witness IPV carry the cycle into adulthood.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**PWDVA 2005** is civil law: protection, residence and monetary orders.`,`**BNSS section 397**: every hospital must treat rape survivors free and inform police.`,`**No FIR is needed** to examine or treat a survivor; BNS 200 punishes refusal.`,`Review after assault at 2 weeks, 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months.`,`After IPV disclosure, book follow-up on a **safe pretext** such as a BP check.`,`Suicidal ideation: do not leave her alone; same-day psychiatry or Tele-MANAS 14416.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs after sexual assault`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Levonorgestrel (EC)`,`1.5 mg oral once`,`Within 72 h (some effect to 120 h)`,`Nausea; repeat if vomited within 2 h`],[`Copper IUD (EC)`,`Intrauterine insertion`,`Within 5 days; can stay 10 years`,`Pain, heavier periods`],[`Azithromycin`,`1 g oral once`,`Single dose`,`Nausea, diarrhoea`],[`Cefixime or ceftriaxone`,`400 mg oral once, or 500 mg IM`,`Single dose`,`Allergy, diarrhoea`],[`Metronidazole`,`2 g oral once`,`Single dose; may defer`,`No alcohol for 48 h`],[`TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)`,`1 tablet oral daily`,`28 days, start within 72 h`,`Nausea, insomnia; separate from iron, calcium`],[`Hepatitis B vaccine ± HBIG`,`IM deltoid; HBIG 0.06 mL/kg`,`0, 1, 6 months`,`Local soreness`],[`Ondansetron`,`4 mg oral`,`30 min before prophylaxis`,`Headache, constipation`],[`Sertraline`,`50 mg oral daily, up to 200 mg`,`6 months after recovery`,`Nausea, agitation; review at 2 weeks`]]},{heading:`Legal duties of the family physician`,columns:[`Law`,`Key provision`,`Doctor's duty`],rows:[[`PWDVA 2005`,`Civil protection and residence orders`,`Give medical aid; report to Protection Officer on request`],[`BNSS 2023 s397`,`Free immediate care for rape and acid-attack survivors`,`Treat free; inform police`],[`BNS 2023 s200`,`Punishment for not treating`,`Never refuse or delay care`],[`BNSS 2023 s184`,`Examination with consent`,`Examine within 24 hours`],[`POCSO Act 2012 s19, s21`,`Mandatory reporting of child abuse`,`Report every case under 18`],[`BNS 2023 s72`,`Survivor identity protected`,`Maintain confidentiality`]]},{heading:`Helplines and services`,columns:[`Service`,`Number or place`,`What it gives`],rows:[[`One Stop Centre (Sakhi)`,`Every district`,`Medical, police, legal aid, counselling, shelter 5 days`],[`Women Helpline`,`181`,`24x7 support and referral`],[`Emergency`,`112`,`Police for imminent danger`],[`Child Helpline`,`1098`,`Child protection`],[`Tele-MANAS`,`14416`,`Mental health support`]]}],remember:[`**LIVES**: Listen, Inquire, Validate, Enhance safety, Support.`,`Ask alone, ask directly, believe her, never blame.`,`Couple counselling is contraindicated while violence continues.`,`**Two-finger test is banned**; absence of injury is not absence of assault.`,`72 hours: **EC, STI prophylaxis, PEP (TLD 28 days)**.`,`No FIR needed for care; BNSS 397 mandates free treatment.`,`POCSO reporting is mandatory under 18.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - IPV and sexual assault`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**IPV**: physical, sexual, emotional, economic abuse or **controlling behaviour** by current/former partner; **sexual violence** by any person in any setting; **'rape' is a legal term decided by a court**, not the doctor.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Types**: physical, sexual, emotional/psychological, economic, structural/cultural (dowry deaths, honour crimes, child marriage, trafficking, acid attacks); **NFHS-5: 29% ever-married women** report spousal physical/sexual violence; NCRB 2022 about **4.45 lakh crimes against women**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Husband's **alcohol use**, low education, poverty, witnessing parental violence, **dowry demands**, controlling attitudes; violence often **starts or escalates in pregnancy**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`**Power and control** -> coercive pattern + **cycle of violence (tension -> assault -> honeymoon)** -> chronic HPA activation -> pain, hypertension, IBS, **PTSD, depression, suicide**; **tonic immobility** - no struggle does not mean consent; **strangulation** may leave no marks.`],[`Key history (OPD)`,`Clues - **injury inconsistent with story, delay, repeat visits, partner answers for her**; **ask alone**, private, interpreter; direct non-judgemental questions; **danger assessment** (threats to kill, weapons, **strangulation**, pregnancy, separation); assault details, LMP, consensual sex within 72 h; suicidality; children.`],[`Key examination`,`**Life-threatening injuries first**; **separate consent** (12 or over consents herself) for exam, evidence, treatment, police; **any RMP**, female preferred but **never delay**; head-to-toe **body map + photos**; neck after strangulation; **two-finger test banned (SC 2022)**; absence of injury does not exclude assault.`],[`Minimal investigations`,`**UPT**; **HIV, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis baseline**, repeat 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months; creatinine before tenofovir (do not delay first dose); **forensic samples within 96 h** (collect whenever she presents); imaging as indicated (CT angiography neck after strangulation with neuro signs).`],[`Treatment - first line (LIVES)`,`**Listen, Inquire, Validate, Enhance safety, Support**; no blame, **no couple counselling**, no pressure to leave; **safety plan** (emergency bag, code word, helplines); document in her words; **free copy of report**.`],[`Three time-critical drugs`,`**EC: LNG 1.5 mg within 72 h** (Cu-IUD within 5 days best); **STI prophylaxis: azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg (or ceftriaxone 500 mg IM) + metronidazole 2 g**; **HIV PEP: TLD (TDF 300 + 3TC 300 + DTG 50) once daily 28 days, start within 72 h**; hepatitis B vaccine 0, 1, 6 months (+ HBIG 0.06 mL/kg if source positive); Td if wounds.`],[`Mental health`,`Psychological first aid; **trauma-focused CBT or EMDR** for PTSD; **sertraline 50-200 mg** for moderate-severe depression; **avoid routine benzodiazepines**; suicidal - do not leave alone, **Tele-MANAS 14416**.`],[`Legal duties (India)`,`**BNSS 2023 s397 - free immediate treatment of rape/acid-attack survivors and inform police**; **BNS s200** punishes refusal; **no FIR needed** to examine/treat; **POCSO s19 mandatory reporting under 18**; **BNS s72 - never disclose identity**; PWDVA 2005 civil protection (Protection Officer, DIR); BNS 85-86 cruelty, s80 dowry death.`],[`Special groups`,`**Children**: POCSO, child-protection pathway; **pregnancy**: abdominal trauma -> abruption, preterm labour; **pregnancy from rape - MTP Amendment Act 2021 up to 24 weeks** (mifepristone 200 mg + misoprostol 800 microgram), preserve fetal tissue for DNA; **burns in young married woman** - dowry suspicion, dying declaration.`],[`Complications`,`Unintended pregnancy, STI/HIV, chronic pain, **PTSD, depression, suicide, homicide**, adverse pregnancy outcomes, effects on children (intergenerational cycle).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Imminent danger - call 112, One Stop Centre shelter, do not send home**; strangulation with LOC/voice change/stridor; suicidal ideation; children at risk; refer **One Stop Centre (Sakhi), Women Helpline 181**, DLSA free legal aid, Protection Officer.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`After assault review at **2 weeks, 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months** (PEP adherence, pregnancy test, serology, vaccine); after IPV disclosure follow up on a **safe pretext**; persistent distress over 1 month - psychiatry.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Mission Shakti - One Stop Centres in every district**, 181 helpline, Shakti Sadan, victim compensation via DLSA; train staff, private room, helpline numbers in women's toilet; engage men and boys, end child marriage.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Treat first, police later - no FIR needed**; **two-finger test is misconduct**; **EC + STI prophylaxis + PEP at one visit within 72 h**; ask alone, never in front of the partner; POCSO reporting is mandatory.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Sexual assault survivor - first visit`,steps:[{label:`Resuscitate and treat injuries`,detail:`Life before evidence; free treatment is a legal duty (BNSS s397)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Consent - separately for each part`,detail:`Exam, evidence, treatment, police intimation; 12 or over consents herself; no FIR needed`,tone:`decision`},{label:`History, body map, photographs`,detail:`Her words in quotes; no two-finger test; no comment on hymen or past sex`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Forensic samples`,detail:`Best within 96 hours, but collect whenever she presents`},{label:`Three drugs within 72 hours`,detail:`LNG 1.5 mg EC; azithromycin 1 g + cefixime 400 mg + metronidazole 2 g; TLD PEP for 28 days`,tone:`good`},{label:`Baseline serology, HBV vaccine, Td`,detail:`HIV, HBsAg, HCV, syphilis; repeat at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months`},{label:`LIVES, safety, referral`,detail:`One Stop Centre, 181, police if she wishes (mandatory under POCSO); follow-up at 2 weeks`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`IPV disclosure - response by danger level`,steps:[{label:`Suspect and ask alone`,detail:`Clinical enquiry when clues present; private room, interpreter, confidentiality limits explained`,tone:`decision`},{label:`LIVES first-line support`,detail:`Listen, Inquire, Validate, Enhance safety, Support - no blame, no couple counselling`,tone:`good`},{label:`Safety plan and documentation`,detail:`Emergency bag, code word, helplines; injuries on body map; free copy of records`},{label:`Link services`,detail:`Protection Officer (PWDVA DIR), One Stop Centre, DLSA, counselling, SHG and skills`},{label:`Imminent danger`,detail:`Threats to kill, weapons, strangulation, escalation -> 112, immediate shelter, never send home`,tone:`warn`}]}]}};export{e as default};